#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""migrate-orient-account — move an existing install's Orient onto its own
account, and rotate its two Library tokens.

    sudo bin/migrate-orient-account --shape two|three --deploy-signals-bound \
        [--root-ca <file> --app-key <file> --app-config <file> --persons <emails>]
                                                           migrate what is pending
    sudo bin/migrate-orient-account --shape two|three --dry-run      say what would happen, change nothing
    sudo bin/migrate-orient-account --shape two|three --check        report only; 1 if migration is pending
    sudo bin/migrate-orient-account --shape two|three --status       the same report, changing nothing

`--root-ca`, `--app-key`, `--app-config` and `--persons` (a comma-separated
list of emails) are the operator's own sources for the Library's person-save
inputs — the same four `bin/place-person-save` reads on the single-account
shape. Omitting any of them skips placing those inputs and says so; every
other step still runs, since saving was already unavailable before this ran.

**THE PROBLEM THIS CLOSES.** An install made before Orient got its own
service account ran Orient as `agents`, with Orient's control database, its
two Library credentials and its own config root sitting under `agents`' own
home, and a LaunchAgent, `com.convivy.orient-deploy-watch`, carrying every
merge to the live site on `agents`' own behalf. This moves Orient onto its
own service account, created by `bin/provision-accounts` if it is not there
yet, places Orient's code there with `bin/place-orient`, rotates
`library-token` and `library-client-token` (which sat where `agents` could
read them) rather than carrying them over, and turns on Orient's signed
document saves.

**`--deploy-signals-bound` IS REQUIRED, AND THIS REFUSES WITHOUT IT.** The
spec's step 6 starts only once Orient's crew has bound the instruction block
that reads `deploy_failure` and `deploy_stall` signals to its own sessions
(step 7), so a stalled deploy is never silent from the first cycle. Whether
that binding has happened is a fact about another crew's session
configuration, which nothing this script can reach names or verifies — there
is no file or command on this machine that answers it. So, as the spec's own
task asks when it gives no check: this is an explicit operator-confirmation
flag. Passing it asserts that Orient's crew has made that binding; the
script trusts the assertion and does not try to re-derive it. It is read
only in a real run — `--dry-run`, `--check` and `--status` need it not, since
they change nothing.

**WHAT IT DOES, IN ORDER.** Nothing runs when `{agents' home}/.orient-control/
control.db` does not exist — a fresh install, or an install already
migrated, changes nothing. Otherwise, each step below is safe to re-run
after an earlier run failed partway, and nothing under `agents` or the
Library is touched until the step before it has fully succeeded:

 1. Check that the root deploy step — `bin/orient-deploy`, declared in
    `jobs/follow-orient-main/orient-deploy.toml`, label
    `com.convivy.orient-deploy` — is loaded in the supervisor, and refuse,
    naming the missing unit, before anything else runs, if it is not. This
    only checks; it does not start the root deploy step yet — see step 17.
 2. On an install that follows Orient's `main` (`install.json` holds
    `"follow_orient_main": true`), read what that step last recorded as
    deployed, `/opt/builder/var/orient-deploy/deployed`, and refuse,
    before anything moves, if it names no commit — nothing else would put
    Orient's own code under its own account before the deploy step's
    first cycle after this migration; see step 13.
 3. Create Orient's own account if it is not there yet
    (`provision-accounts`).
 4. Repoint the operator's `cloudflared` setting at Orient's own socket
    and restart `cloudflared`, before any of Orient's jobs stop, so the
    tunnel never forwards to Orient's old loopback address while Orient is
    down.
 5. Stop Orient's three units (`com.convivy.orient`, `.orient-dashboard`,
    `.orient-backup`) wherever they are currently loaded, and take out of
    the supervisor whichever of their definitions still runs as `agents` or
    out of `agents`' own deploy path — the same defect, and the same fix,
    `bin/migrate-library-account` already carries for the Library's units.
 6. Remove the old `com.convivy.orient-deploy-watch` LaunchAgent (or, on
    Linux, its systemd --user unit), which carried every merge to the live
    site on `agents`' own behalf, keeping its own bytes in memory until the
    rotation so a rollback can write it back and reload it.
 7. Place Orient's own code (`place-orient`) — on an install that follows
    Orient's `main`, this places only the `{bin}` links, leaving Orient's
    own code to step 13 below.
 8. **THE POINT OF NO RETURN.** Rotate `library-token` and
    `library-client-token` with `mint_credential.py --replace`, reading
    each entry's own fields directly from its token store first (the
    store's own secret key is never one of them) and passing them back
    unchanged but for the new secret, then refusing unless they still
    agree afterward. This runs entirely against the Library's own
    account's token stores and `.orient-control` — never `agents`' —
    because `deliver-library-credentials` already delivers Orient's
    credentials there rather than to `agents`.
 9. Mint Orient's own narrow signal credential, `capture-token` (holding
    `signal.create` alone — see `bin/orient-deploy`'s own `post_signal`),
    into the Library account's own `.orient-control`, unless it is already
    there: the same idempotent mint a fresh install's `jobs/library.toml`
    own `prepare` runs, so a machine already carrying it from that mint
    never mints a second one. Before deciding whether to mint
    `kb-person-save-token`, re-mint it with no crew in place, with
    `mint_credential.py --replace`, when the store already carries it under
    a crew — see `remint_kb_person_save_crew`'s own docstring for why a
    crew can make it authenticate nobody. Otherwise mint
    `kb-person-save-token` the same idempotent way `capture-token` is
    minted, unless `agents` already has a copy — see
    `mint_kb_person_save_token`'s own docstring for why `agents`' own copy,
    not the Library's, is the guard there. Deliver both `capture-token` and
    `kb-person-save-token`, together with the two rotated tokens, to
    Orient's own account and to `agents` with
    `bin/deliver-library-credentials`, then delete `library-token` and
    `library-client-token`'s old, now-superseded copies from `agents`' own
    `.orient-control`, and `agents`' own old, wide `capture-token` — minted
    under the name `github-capture`, with `kind` system and `products`
    `"*"` — where one is there. Absence changes nothing; the operator
    still revokes its credential by hand, once `orient-deploy` is seen
    posting with the new, narrow credential, since deleting this file is
    not revoking it.
10. Move every other file `.orient-control` and Orient's config root hold —
    `control.db`, `vapid.json`, `orient-serving-commit`, `orient-restart-
    started`, `journal-push-token`, `journal-dispatch-token`, and, under the
    config root, `jwt.json`, `tiers.json`, `tailnet.json`, `roles.json`,
    `api-tokens.json` — into Orient's own account, verified the same
    symlink-safe, manifest-checked way `bin/migrate-library-account` moves
    the Library's state. A failure here names every file this call already
    copied and every one of the table above still under `agents`' own
    home, so the operator knows exactly what to delete by hand once
    Orient's own copy is confirmed.
11. Move the sibling logs (`orient.log`, `orient-dashboard.log`,
    `orient-dashboard.err.log`, `orient-backup.log`), as best-effort
    cleanup after the state is already promoted.
12. Remove `agents`' now-unused `~/.local/deploy/convivy-orient`.
13. On an install that follows Orient's `main`, place the commit step 2
    read, extracted from root's own bare copy exactly as `bin/orient-
    deploy` extracts and places a commit, under Orient's own deploy area —
    nothing else would, until the root deploy step's own first cycle after
    this migration (see step 7).
14. Run `bin/orient-make-statement-key` as Orient's own account, from the
    tree root `place-orient` placed, with the Python in Orient's own
    deploy area (never root's system Python, which has no `cryptography`
    and which the tool refuses to run under), and place its one line of
    output under `orient_statement_key` in the operator's own
    `kb-verifier.json`, leaving any `persons` entry already there
    untouched. Place the Library's seven person-save inputs the same way —
    `--root-ca`, `--app-key`, `--app-config` and `--persons`, through a
    child dropped to the Library account's own uid and gid, reusing
    `bin/place-person-save`'s own placement logic — writing the SAME line
    this step just captured, held in memory, into `orient-statement-key.json`
    too, never read back from `kb-verifier.json` or any other file. Skipped,
    with a report saying so, when any of the four is not given.
15. Deploy the shared agent-platform area the document service runs
    `agentctl` from, `~/.local/deploy/agent-platform-shared` under `agents`'
    own home, with the Bosun's `install-agent-platform-shared`, run as
    `agents`, before the document service is installed. Then install the
    document service with Orient's own group as `--client-group`, create
    its socket directory, and load its LaunchDaemon. Both refuse by name on
    Linux, because neither has an installation design for that platform yet.
16. Restart Orient, which is also what makes it start using the document
    service: `jobs/orient.toml`'s own `[account-environment]` names
    `ORIENT_KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET`, read fresh on each call by convivy-orient's
    own `kb_commit.py`, only while Orient's own account is in force, so this
    restart is the step that moves saving onto the service step 15 just
    installed rather than the in-process route that stops working the
    moment Orient leaves `agents`. First renders every declared Orient unit
    from the root tree's declarations as Orient's own, since the agents'
    units were already removed in step 5 and `install-jobs --start` only
    activates a unit already on disk, then starts every one of them; a
    rollback, by contrast, restarts only the agents' units this install
    actually had, so an install made before `com.convivy.orient-backup`
    existed is never asked to start that one.
17. Only once that restart reports no problem, start the root deploy step
    that step 1 already checked is loaded — never earlier, since starting it
    before Orient's own system unit exists is exactly what fails against an
    install whose Orient `main` is ahead of the deployed commit: the cycle
    places the new commit, then its restart of `com.convivy.orient.service`
    fails, `Unit not found`, and the swap-back fails the same way. This is
    already past the point of no return, so a failure here is reported, not
    rolled back, and never leaves the timer or the service running — see
    `start_root_deploy_step` below.

**ROLLBACK.** A failure any time before the two tokens are rotated (step 8
above) restores whatever unit this run removed and restarts whatever this
run stopped, and leaves `agents`' own files untouched — so a failure there
always leaves the old tokens working, as the task that commissioned this
script requires. Once cloudflared's config has been written (step 4),
rollback puts the original setting back whether or not that step went on
to validate or restart cloudflared itself, naming what it restored rather
than letting the failure's own "nothing further was migrated" stand
uncorrected. Before any of Orient's units have stopped — a
`validate_cloudflared` or `restart_cloudflared` refusal inside step 4
itself — the old, agents'-own Orient is still serving the port throughout,
so rollback writes the file back directly, restarting cloudflared again
only if it had already been restarted onto the new setting. Once Orient's
units have stopped, rollback restarts cloudflared only once the restored,
agents'-own Orient is confirmed to hold its old loopback port again: the
process listening on it, read with `lsof`, has to be the main process the
system supervisor itself reports for the restored `com.convivy.orient`
unit, and a connection to the port has to succeed. An impostor bound to
that port — any process the supervisor does not report as that unit's own
— fails the check exactly as no listener at all does, and the tunnel is
left pointed at Orient's own socket, naming whatever holds the port, when
something does, rather than ever revert onto a port that account does not
really own. A failure from step 8 onward is cleanup, not reversible: the new account
already holds verified, promoted data by then, so this reports what it
could not finish rather than unwinding what already succeeded, the same
split `bin/migrate-library-account` already draws between its own
verified-and-promoted state move and the best-effort log move after it;
code already placed (the root tree, and Orient's own deploy area) is left
for the next run, since placing it again changes nothing.

**THE ROOT DEPLOY STEP THIS STARTS.** The design calls this "the root
deploy step" — a root-owned LaunchDaemon or systemd timer that fetches,
verifies and places
each signed merge to Orient's `main`, replacing the `com.convivy.orient-
deploy-watch` LaunchAgent this script retires. That step is `bin/orient-
deploy`, declared in `jobs/follow-orient-main/orient-deploy.toml`, label
`com.convivy.orient-deploy` — the same label `ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL` below
already names, and the same state file, `/opt/builder/run/orient-deploy/
state.json`, `ROOT_DEPLOY_STATE_DIR` already names. That tool and job land
through their own change, a sibling of this one rather than something this
one builds on, so an install this runs against may not have enabled it yet.
Step 1 above checks whether the unit is actually loaded before relying on
it, and refuses cleanly, naming the missing unit, rather than assuming it
is there — see `root_deploy_step_loaded` below — but does not start it
until step 17, once Orient's own units are confirmed running again; see
`start_root_deploy_step` below.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import errno
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import plistlib
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import socket
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

PROG = "migrate-orient-account"
HERE_FILE = Path(__file__).resolve()
REPO = HERE_FILE.parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / "tools"))
from jobs import declaration  # noqa: E402

# `bin/install-privileged`'s own `running_copy()` (see "refusing to run
# from" there) is the ownership check: `main()`'s real-run path loads
# that sibling through `_install_privileged_for_extract()` right after
# this script's own path check passes, and treats the `SystemExit` its
# `running_copy()` raises as this script's own refusal — so the two
# cannot disagree about ownership of `prefix`, `prefix/src`, the
# extracted tree root, the `bin` directory, install-privileged's own
# file, or its two further siblings (`github-gate`,
# `account_entities.py`). `running_copy()` checks its OWN file's path,
# never this one's, so `script_location_problem` below still checks,
# on its own, that this file sits at the expected
# `<prefix>/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/migrate-orient-account` shape —
# the one thing loading the sibling does not cover.
RUNNING = re.compile(r"^(?P<prefix>.*)/src/shipwright@(?P<sha>[0-9a-f]{40})/bin/migrate-orient-account$")


def script_location_problem(here_file: Path) -> str | None:
    """Whether `here_file` has the shape
    `<prefix>/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/migrate-orient-account`, or why
    it does not. Called only in the real-run path, in `main()`, before
    `migrate()` changes anything — never at import time. Ownership and
    writability of the tree this file sits in are checked separately,
    by loading install-privileged's own `running_copy()` (see the
    comment above `RUNNING`); this function covers only this script's
    own path, which that check does not.
    """
    if not RUNNING.match(str(here_file)):
        return "the file is not at <prefix>/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/migrate-orient-account"
    return None


def _load_sibling(module_name: str, filename: str):
    """`filename`, from this file's own directory, loaded under
    `module_name` rather than `import`ed by path. Mirrors `bin/orient-
    deploy`'s own helper of the same name."""
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(module_name, str(REPO / "bin" / filename))
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(module_name, loader))
    sys.modules[module_name] = module
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


# `bin/place-orient`'s own `place_argv()`, reused here rather than
# re-derived, for `place_deployed_commit` below. Safe to load eagerly,
# unlike `bin/install-privileged` (see `_extract_deployed_tree` below):
# place-orient does no work and no path check at import time, only
# function and class definitions.
place_orient = _load_sibling("place_orient_for_migrate_orient_account", "place-orient")

# `bin/place-person-save`'s own `place_entries()`, reused here rather than
# duplicated, for `place_library_person_save_inputs` below: same safety
# reasoning as `place_orient` above — this does no work and no path check at
# import time, only function and class definitions.
place_person_save = _load_sibling("place_person_save_for_migrate_orient_account", "place-person-save")

AGENTS = "agents"
# Duplicated from `bin/provision-accounts`' own tables rather than imported,
# the same way `bin/migrate-library-account` already duplicates its own.
LIBRARY_ACCOUNT_NAMES = {"macos": "_library", "linux": "library"}
ORIENT_ACCOUNT_NAMES = {"macos": "_orient", "linux": "orient"}

SUDO = "/usr/bin/sudo"
# The same interpreters `bin/stand-up-backend`'s own `ROOT_INTERPRETERS`
# names, for the root-owned copies of `provision-accounts`, `place-orient`,
# `deliver-library-credentials` and `install-jobs` this runs beside.
ROOT_INTERPRETERS = {"darwin": "/opt/homebrew/bin/python3.11", "linux": "/usr/bin/python3"}
PRIVILEGED_PREFIX = Path("/opt/builder")


def root_interpreter(platform: str) -> str:
    """`ROOT_INTERPRETERS`, keyed by `sys.platform`'s own spelling
    ("darwin"/"linux"), indexed by this file's own "macos"/"linux"
    `platform` strings. `bin/stand-up-backend`'s own call sites re-derive
    the dict key from `sys.platform` directly rather than from a "macos"/
    "linux" variable, for exactly this reason."""
    return ROOT_INTERPRETERS["darwin" if platform == "macos" else "linux"]

# The three Orient units `jobs/orient.toml`, `jobs/orient-dashboard.toml` and
# `jobs/orient-backup.toml` declare.
ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS = ("com.convivy.orient", "com.convivy.orient-dashboard", "com.convivy.orient-backup")
# Which of the three schedules a recurring run, and so gets a sibling
# `.timer` on Linux (see `tools/jobs/render_systemd.needs_timer`): the main
# job runs continuously (`at-load` alone) and has none; the dashboard build
# and the backup each declare a schedule of their own. Duplicated here
# rather than read from the declaration, the same way
# `bin/migrate-library-account` already duplicates its own equivalent.
ORIENT_UNITS_WITH_TIMER = frozenset({"com.convivy.orient-dashboard", "com.convivy.orient-backup"})
SYSTEMD_SYSTEM_DIR = "/etc/systemd/system"
LAUNCHD_SYSTEM_DIR = "/Library/LaunchDaemons"

# THE OLD WATCHER THIS RETIRES. A LaunchAgent (a per-user, not system-scope,
# job) that ran as `agents` and carried every merge to the live site on its
# own behalf. No file under `jobs/` declares it, because it was never one of
# Builder's own job declarations; it is removed by name alone.
OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL = "com.convivy.orient-deploy-watch"

# THE NEW ROOT DEPLOY STEP THIS STARTS IN ITS PLACE. Not yet shipped by this
# release — see the module docstring's own note. Named here as the one
# concrete thing this script can check for and act on: a unit already
# loaded under this label, root-owned, the way every other root-run
# supervisor unit in this codebase is found.
ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL = "com.convivy.orient-deploy"
ROOT_DEPLOY_STATE_DIR = PRIVILEGED_PREFIX / "run" / "orient-deploy"

STATE_REL = Path(".local", "state")
ORIENT_CONTROL = ".orient-control"
ORIENT_CONFIG_REL = Path(".local", "state", "orient")
DEPLOY_ORIENT_REL = Path(".local", "deploy", "convivy-orient")
BIN_REL = Path(".local", "bin")

# The two Library credentials Orient's own account receives and rotates —
# duplicated from `bin/deliver-library-credentials`' own `ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`.
ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES = ("library-token", "library-client-token")
# `mint_credential.py`'s own `--file` value for each of the two, in the same
# order as `ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES` — the agents-type store for the first, the
# clients-type store for the second, matching `jobs/library.toml`'s own
# `prepare` entries for `library-token`/`library-client-token`.
ORIENT_TOKEN_STORE_FILES = ("agents", "clients")
ORIENT_MINT_NAME = "orient"
# The on-disk file each of `ORIENT_TOKEN_STORE_FILES` names, under the
# Library account's own state directory — `jobs/library.toml`'s own
# `LIBRARY_API_TOKENS`/`LIBRARY_CLIENT_TOKENS` (`{state}/library/
# api-tokens.json` and `.../client-tokens.json`), duplicated here rather
# than read from that job's environment, the same way every other store
# name in this file is duplicated rather than imported.
ORIENT_TOKEN_STORE_FILENAMES = {"agents": "api-tokens.json", "clients": "client-tokens.json"}

# Orient's own narrow signal credential — never rotated, since it does not
# exist on an install this script has not already touched; see
# `mint_capture_token` below. Duplicated from `jobs/library.toml`'s own
# `prepare` mint of the same name, the same way every other credential name
# and store path in this file is duplicated rather than imported.
CAPTURE_TOKEN_NAME = "capture-token"
CAPTURE_TOKEN_MINT_PREFIX = "orient-deploy-signals-"
# Every agent capability but `signal.create`, withheld from the `agent` role
# `mint_credential.py --kind agent` would otherwise grant in full. Computed at
# mint time, in `library_role_withholds` below, never written out here as a
# tuple: see that function for why.
CAPTURE_TOKEN_GRANT = "signal.create"

# The credential the document service — running as `agents` — presents to the
# Library's person-save route on Orient's behalf, read from
# `~/.orient-control/kb-person-save-token` by the Bosun's `agentctl kb apply`.
# A fresh install's `jobs/library.toml` own `prepare` mints this on every
# shape, and `deliver-library-credentials` already routes it to `agents` as
# one of `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`; this install may predate that mint, so this
# script mints it too, the same idempotent way it mints `capture-token`, when
# `agents` still has none. Holds `reference.person_save` alone: every other
# agent capability, `signal.create` included, is withheld — computed the same
# way as `capture-token`'s own, in `library_role_withholds` below. Minted with
# no crew: the Library resolves a crew credential's entity through its own
# project registry, and a crew with no row there makes the Library refuse the
# credential with "no resolvable identity", so a token minted for a crew one
# install has and another lacks authenticates nobody on the second. An
# install that minted it with a crew is corrected in place by
# `remint_kb_person_save_crew` below.
KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME = "kb-person-save-token"
KB_PERSON_SAVE_GRANT = "reference.person_save"

# Every other file `.orient-control` and the config root hold for Orient,
# duplicated here rather than read from Orient's own code, the same way
# `bin/migrate-library-account` duplicates its own credential names rather
# than importing them. NEVER `capture-token`: that name is `agents`' own old,
# wide signal credential (minted under the store name `github-capture`),
# left where it is rather than moved — see `mint_capture_token` below, which
# mints Orient's own narrow replacement instead.
ORIENT_CONTROL_OTHER_NAMES = (
    "control.db", "vapid.json", "orient-serving-commit", "orient-restart-started",
    "journal-push-token", "journal-dispatch-token",
)

# Named here only for `remaining_agents_orient_files` below's own report —
# the config root's own move is still the whole-tree `copy_tree` a few
# names down, which copies whatever is actually there, not only these five.
ORIENT_CONFIG_ROOT_NAMES = ("jwt.json", "tiers.json", "tailnet.json", "roles.json", "api-tokens.json")

# The sibling logs `jobs/orient.toml`, `orient-dashboard.toml` and
# `orient-backup.toml` each write directly under `{state}`, moved the same
# best-effort way `bin/migrate-library-account` moves `library.log`.
ORIENT_SIBLING_LOGS = ("orient.log", "orient-dashboard.log", "orient-dashboard.err.log", "orient-backup.log")

# Where `bin/orient-make-statement-key` writes the key this run creates, and
# the operator's own verifier file it gets placed into.
STATEMENT_KEY_NAME = "kb-statement-key.pem"
KB_VERIFIER_REL = Path(".config", "convivy", "kb-verifier.json")

# The document service's own socket directory and group. `install-kb-document-
# api` itself loads nothing and runs no sudo: it only prints the root commands
# below for an operator to run by hand, which `install_document_service`
# below performs itself, as root, rather than leaving them printed.
KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_DIR = Path("/var/db/convivy-kb-documents")
KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_NAME = "kb-documents.sock"
# setgid + 0750. The setgid bit is what makes a socket bind() under this
# directory inherit its group, which `mkdir`'s own mode argument cannot set,
# so it is applied with a separate `chmod`.
KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_MODE = 0o2750
KB_DOCUMENT_API_LABEL = "com.convivy.kb-document-api"
# How long `install_document_service` keeps retrying the LaunchDaemon's
# bootstrap after booting the previous one out: 20 tries, half a second apart.
BOOTSTRAP_ATTEMPTS = 20
BOOTSTRAP_RETRY_SECONDS = 0.5
# `install-kb-document-api` stages its LaunchDaemon variant under the
# deploy area it builds for itself, running as `agents` — never where this
# script's own root steps could be mistaken for what it printed.
KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_PLIST_REL = Path(".local", "deploy", "kb-document-api",
                                    f"{KB_DOCUMENT_API_LABEL}.daemon-example.plist")
# 23 decimal is octal 027, the umask the document service's design calls
# for. launchd.plist(5) stores `Umask` as a decimal integer whose digits
# read as octal.
KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_UMASK = 23
# The only keys `install-kb-document-api` itself ever writes into this
# plist: `Label` and `ProgramArguments` come from its own template, and
# `UserName`, `GroupName` and `Umask` are the three it appends for the
# LaunchDaemon variant. `verify_staged_daemon_plist` refuses anything else —
# `Sockets` or `MachServices` named here would hand `agents` a privileged
# bind that launchd performs on root's own signature of this unit.
KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_PLIST_ALLOWED_KEYS = frozenset(
    {"Label", "ProgramArguments", "UserName", "GroupName", "Umask"})

# The keys install-kb-document-api copies into the daemon plist unchanged from
# agent-platform's own launchd template, where `__HOME__` is the only
# substitution. Each is accepted only when it equals the value root renders
# itself from its own placed tree's copy of that template, never on the
# staged plist's say-so: `agents` writes the staged plist, and a log path or
# working directory it chose would be opened by launchd for this job.
KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_TEMPLATE_KEYS = frozenset(
    {"WorkingDirectory", "RunAtLoad", "KeepAlive", "StandardOutPath", "StandardErrorPath",
     "EnvironmentVariables"})
KB_DOCUMENT_API_TEMPLATE_REL = Path("launchd", "com.convivy.kb-document-api.plist")

# The operator's own `cloudflared` configuration, and the service running
# it: `cloudflared` stays the operator's. The label is this install's own
# choice, not read from any file; `--cloudflared-label` overrides it for an
# install that named it differently.
CLOUDFLARED_CONFIG_REL = Path(".cloudflared", "config.yml")
DEFAULT_CLOUDFLARED_LABEL = "com.cloudflare.cloudflared"
ORIENT_SOCKET_PATH = "/opt/builder/run/orient/orient.sock"

# The old loopback address `repoint_cloudflared`'s own ingress rule match
# names (`http://127.0.0.1:8099`) — the port a rollback confirms is truly
# held by the restored, agents'-own Orient before writing that line back,
# with `lsof`.
ORIENT_OLD_LOOPBACK_PORT = 8099
LSOF = "/usr/sbin/lsof"

# Whether this install follows Orient's main, and what root's deploy step
# last recorded as deployed — read for `place_deployed_commit` below,
# duplicated from `bin/place-orient`'s own `follows_orient_main` and
# `bin/orient-deploy`'s own `deployed_commit`/`DEPLOYED` respectively,
# rather than imported: neither is part of either tool's own public shape.
INSTALL_CONFIG_NAME = "install.json"
FOLLOW_ORIENT_MAIN_KEY = "follow_orient_main"
ORIENT_DEPLOY_RECORD_REL = Path("var", "orient-deploy", "deployed")
CONVIVY_ORIENT_REPO = "convivy-orient"
SHA = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{40}")

ROOT_UID = 0
ROOT_TOP = Path("/")
DIR_OPEN = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW
FILE_OPEN = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW
NEW_FILE = os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW


class Refused(Exception):
    """Something the operator has to decide about. Never a stack trace."""


# --- accounts, duplicated from bin/migrate-library-account --------------------


def library_account_name(platform: str | None = None) -> str:
    platform = platform or ("macos" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux")
    return LIBRARY_ACCOUNT_NAMES[platform]


def orient_account_name(platform: str | None = None) -> str:
    platform = platform or ("macos" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux")
    return ORIENT_ACCOUNT_NAMES[platform]


def account_home(name: str) -> str | None:
    try:
        return pwd.getpwnam(name).pw_dir
    except KeyError:
        return None


def account_ids(name: str) -> tuple[int, int] | None:
    try:
        entry = pwd.getpwnam(name)
    except KeyError:
        return None
    return entry.pw_uid, entry.pw_gid


def user_manager_argv(account: str, uid: int, *args: str) -> list[str]:
    """argv for a `systemctl --user` call against `account`'s own user
    manager, run as `account` via `sudo` from root. `sudo -H` clears the
    environment, so `systemctl --user` has nothing to find that manager
    by without `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` supplied here from `uid` directly."""
    return [SUDO, "-u", account, "-H", "env", f"XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/{uid}",
            "systemctl", "--user", *args]


def owned_by(path: str, uid: int) -> bool:
    """Whether `path` exists, is a real directory and is owned by `uid`.

    Never follows a symlink: a symlink at an account's home would otherwise
    read or write through it to wherever it points.
    """
    try:
        info = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return True  # nothing there yet is not foreign; it just does not exist
    if stat.S_ISLNK(info.st_mode):
        return False
    return info.st_uid == uid


def _owned_by(path: Path, owner: int) -> str | None:
    """Duplicated from `bin/place-orient`'s own version of this function."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return f"{path} is missing"
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode) or st.st_uid != owner or st.st_mode & 0o022:
        return f"{path} is not owned by uid {owner} alone, or another account can write it"
    return None


def ancestry_problem(path: Path, owner: int, top: Path) -> str | None:
    """Why `path` is not trusted as `owner`'s alone, or None when it is.

    Every component from `path` up to and including `top` must belong to
    `owner` with no group or other write bit and must not be a symlink.
    Duplicated from `bin/place-orient`'s own version of this function.
    """
    for part in [path, *path.parents]:
        if part == top or top in part.parents:
            problem = _owned_by(part, owner)
            if problem:
                return problem
    return None


def mkdir_owned(path: Path, uid: int, gid: int) -> None:
    """Create `path` and every missing ancestor below one that already
    exists, each private (`0700`) and owned by `(uid, gid)`. Duplicated from
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of this function."""
    if path.exists():
        return
    mkdir_owned(path.parent, uid, gid)
    os.mkdir(path, 0o700)
    os.chown(path, uid, gid)


# --- forking and dropping privileges, duplicated from the same tool -----------


def _drop_privileges(uid: int, gid: int) -> None:
    """Become `(uid, gid)` in THIS process, with no supplementary groups
    carried over. Called only in a child that is about to do nothing else
    but read or write what that account owns, or run one command as it."""
    os.setgroups([])
    os.setgid(gid)
    os.setuid(uid)


def run_as_account(uid: int, gid: int, action, *, drop=None):
    """Run `action()` — a zero-argument callable returning a JSON-able
    value — in a child that has dropped to `(uid, gid)` first, and return
    what it returns, or raise `Refused` with what it raised. Duplicated
    from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of this function."""
    if drop is None:
        drop = _drop_privileges
    read_fd, write_fd = os.pipe()
    sys.stdout.flush()
    sys.stderr.flush()
    pid = os.fork()
    if pid == 0:
        os.close(read_fd)
        try:
            drop(uid, gid)
            result = action()
            payload = json.dumps({"ok": True, "result": result}).encode("utf-8")
        except BaseException as exc:
            payload = json.dumps({"ok": False, "error": str(exc)}).encode("utf-8")
        try:
            with os.fdopen(write_fd, "wb") as out:
                out.write(len(payload).to_bytes(8, "big"))
                out.write(payload)
        finally:
            os._exit(0)
    os.close(write_fd)
    with os.fdopen(read_fd, "rb") as inp:
        header = inp.read(8)
        size = int.from_bytes(header, "big") if header else 0
        data = inp.read(size) if size else b""
    os.waitpid(pid, 0)
    decoded = json.loads(data.decode("utf-8")) if data else {"ok": False, "error": "the child reported nothing"}
    if not decoded.get("ok"):
        raise Refused(decoded.get("error") or "the child failed silently")
    return decoded.get("result")


# --- detection ------------------------------------------------------------------


def agents_orient_control(agents_home: Path) -> Path:
    return agents_home / ORIENT_CONTROL


def needs_migration(agents_home: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether `agents` still holds Orient's own control database.

    True only for an install whose Orient used to run as `agents`. A fresh
    install, and an install already migrated (the source was removed once
    the copy was verified), both answer False, so a rerun changes nothing.
    `control.db`, not the `.orient-control` directory itself, marks a real
    Orient: `jobs/orient.toml`'s own `directories` creates that directory
    for Orient's first start to use, and the Library's own `prepare` mints
    `library-token`/`library-client-token` into it on `agents` too, on an
    install that has never run Orient at all — the same reasoning
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own `needs_migration` already applies to
    the Library's `library.db`.
    """
    db = agents_orient_control(agents_home) / "control.db"
    try:
        info = os.lstat(db)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return False
    return stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode)


# --- accounts and units, called as subprocesses -------------------------------


def ensure_orient_account(shape: str, platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> bool:
    """Create Orient's own account for `shape`, if it is missing. Returns
    whether it was created. Calls `bin/provision-accounts`, the same copy
    this tool ships beside, rather than duplicating what it already does."""
    name = orient_account_name(platform)
    if account_home(name) is not None:
        return False
    tool = REPO / "bin" / "provision-accounts"
    interpreter = root_interpreter(platform)
    done = run([interpreter, str(tool), "--shape", shape],
               capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not create the {name} account: provision-accounts --shape {shape} exited "
            f"{done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing was migrated.")
    if account_home(name) is None:
        raise Refused(
            f"provision-accounts --shape {shape} ran but {name} still does not exist. "
            f"Nothing was migrated.")
    return True


def place_orient_code(platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Place Orient's own code and `{bin}` links with `bin/place-orient`,
    the same copy this tool ships beside. Idempotent by that tool's own
    design, so calling it again on a rerun changes nothing once it has
    already succeeded."""
    tool = REPO / "bin" / "place-orient"
    interpreter = root_interpreter(platform)
    done = run([interpreter, str(tool)], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode not in (0, 1):
        raise Refused(
            f"could not place Orient's own code: place-orient exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing was migrated.")


def agents_builder_root(agents_home: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str:
    """The Builder root `agents` runs with, read by `agents` from its own
    linked command. Duplicated from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own
    version of this function."""
    link = f"{agents_home}/.local/bin/builder-root"
    done = run([SUDO, "-u", AGENTS, "-H", link], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    root = (done.stdout or "").strip()
    if done.returncode != 0 or not os.path.isabs(root) or "\n" in root:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not read the Builder root from {link} as {AGENTS}. The link step places "
            f"that command, so it has to have run already. Nothing was migrated.")
    return root


def _install_jobs_argv(agents_home: str, root: str, platform: str) -> list[str]:
    interpreter = root_interpreter(platform)
    return [
        interpreter, str(REPO / "bin" / "install-jobs"),
        "--home", agents_home, "--root", root,
        "--jobs", str(REPO / "jobs"),
        "--log-dir", str(PRIVILEGED_PREFIX / "var" / "log"),
    ]


def _install_jobs_output(done) -> str:
    """Both streams of an `install-jobs` run, stdout first. `install-jobs`
    prints why a job failed (a start that did not leave it running, a
    stop timeout) on stdout, while systemctl's own lines go to stderr, so
    reporting only one stream can drop the reason a step failed."""
    return "\n".join(s.strip() for s in (done.stdout or "", done.stderr or "") if s.strip())


def stop_orient_units(agents_home: str, root: str, platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Stop Orient's three units wherever they are currently loaded.
    Idempotent: a unit that is not running is reported and counts as
    stopped. Mirrors `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own `stop_library_units`."""
    argv = _install_jobs_argv(agents_home, root, platform) + ["--stop", *ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS]
    done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not stop Orient's units before moving its data: install-jobs --stop "
            f"exited {done.returncode}: {_install_jobs_output(done)}. Nothing was migrated.")


def render_orient_units(agents_home: str, root: str, platform: str, privileged_prefix: Path,
                        run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """Render Orient's own units from the root tree's declarations, writing
    their unit files to disk without starting or activating any of them —
    an `install-jobs` run with none of `--activate`, `--stop`, `--start`,
    `--check`, `--status` or `--dry-run`. `--only ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS` keeps
    this from also walking every other declared job's prepare steps (as
    their own privileged accounts) the way an unscoped render did: an
    unrelated job's prepare failure stopped Orient's own units ever being
    written, and was then misreported as Orient's own problem. Install-jobs
    merges `--only`'s record entries into the rest, so the other jobs' own
    entries are kept rather than read as retired. `start_orient_units` below
    only runs `install-jobs --start`, which activates a unit already on disk
    and renders nothing; by the end of the migration, `remove_agents_units`
    has already deleted the agents' own unit files, so nothing puts Orient's
    own on disk unless this runs first. `--record` is required: a root run
    refuses to keep its own record under `agents_home`, which is
    `install-jobs`' default location for it. Never raises."""
    argv = _install_jobs_argv(agents_home, root, platform) + [
        "--only", *ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS,
        "--record", str(privileged_prefix / "var" / "install-jobs" / "units.json"),
    ]
    try:
        done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    except OSError as exc:
        return str(exc)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return f"install-jobs exited {done.returncode}: {_install_jobs_output(done)}"
    return None


def start_orient_units(agents_home: str, root: str, platform: str, labels: tuple[str, ...],
                       run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """Start Orient's units named in `labels`. Rollback passes only the units
    this install actually has (`existing_orient_labels` below, read once the
    agents' units are restored): `install-jobs --start` refuses the whole
    batch, naming it, when asked for a label whose unit file is not there,
    which is exactly what asking for `com.convivy.orient-backup` on an
    install made before that unit existed would do. `install-jobs --start`
    only activates a unit already on disk; it renders nothing. The end of
    the migration passes `ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS` itself, having already
    rendered them with `render_orient_units` above, since by then the
    agents' units are gone. An empty `labels` starts nothing, since
    `install-jobs --start` needs at least one label. Never raises. Mirrors
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own `start_library_units`."""
    if not labels:
        return None
    argv = _install_jobs_argv(agents_home, root, platform) + ["--start", *labels]
    try:
        done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    except OSError as exc:
        return str(exc)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return f"install-jobs --start exited {done.returncode}: {_install_jobs_output(done)}"
    return None


# --- the units an old install left running as agents, duplicated ---------------


def unit_paths(label: str, platform: str, system_dir: str) -> list[Path]:
    """Every file `label`'s unit occupies in `system_dir`. Duplicated from
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of this function."""
    if platform == "macos":
        return [Path(system_dir) / f"{label}.plist"]
    paths = [Path(system_dir) / f"{label}.service"]
    if label in ORIENT_UNITS_WITH_TIMER:
        paths.append(Path(system_dir) / f"{label}.timer")
    return paths


def existing_orient_labels(platform: str, system_dir: str) -> list[str]:
    """Which of `ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS` this install actually has a unit file
    for, under `system_dir` — what rollback passes `start_orient_units`,
    rather than `ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS` outright, once the agents' units are
    restored. An
    install made before `com.convivy.orient-backup` existed never has that
    one, and `install-jobs --start` refuses the whole batch, naming it,
    when asked for a label whose unit file is not there."""
    return [label for label in ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS if unit_paths(label, platform, system_dir)[0].exists()]


def unit_names_agents(data: bytes, platform: str, agents_home: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether a unit's own `.service` or `.plist` bytes still run it as
    `agents` or out of `agents`' own deploy path, rather than Orient's own
    account. Duplicated from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of
    this function, against `DEPLOY_ORIENT_REL` in place of the Library's."""
    deploy = str(agents_home / DEPLOY_ORIENT_REL)
    if platform == "macos":
        plist = plistlib.loads(data)
        return plist.get("UserName") == AGENTS or str(plist.get("WorkingDirectory", "")).startswith(deploy)
    for line in data.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if stripped == f"User={AGENTS}" or stripped.startswith(f"WorkingDirectory={deploy}"):
            return True
    return False


def remove_agents_units(agents_home: Path, platform: str, system_dir: str,
                         removed: dict[Path, bytes], run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Take out of the supervisor every one of Orient's three units that
    still names `agents` or its deploy path. Duplicated from
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of this function, against
    `ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS` in place of the Library's."""
    for label in ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS:
        paths = unit_paths(label, platform, system_dir)
        try:
            primary_data = paths[0].read_bytes()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        if not unit_names_agents(primary_data, platform, agents_home):
            continue
        if platform == "macos":
            run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"system/{label}"], capture_output=True, check=False)
        else:
            for path in paths:
                run(["systemctl", "disable", "--now", path.name], capture_output=True, check=False)
        removed[paths[0]] = primary_data
        paths[0].unlink()
        for path in paths[1:]:
            try:
                removed[path] = path.read_bytes()
            except FileNotFoundError:
                continue
            path.unlink()
    if platform == "linux" and removed and shutil.which("systemctl"):
        run(["systemctl", "daemon-reload"], capture_output=True, check=False)


def restore_agents_units(removed: dict[Path, bytes]) -> None:
    """Write every unit `remove_agents_units` removed back to its original
    path, byte for byte. Duplicated from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own
    version of this function."""
    for path, data in removed.items():
        path.write_bytes(data)


# --- the old deploy watcher, a per-user LaunchAgent/systemd --user unit --------


def deploy_watch_paths(platform: str, agents_home: Path) -> list[Path]:
    """Where `OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL`'s own declaration lives: a LaunchAgent
    under `agents`' own `~/Library/LaunchAgents` on macOS, or, on Linux, the
    matching per-user systemd unit under `agents`' own
    `~/.config/systemd/user` — never confirmed on Linux, since the watcher
    this retires was built ad hoc for a Mac; kept generic here so a Linux
    install carrying an equivalent by the same name is still cleaned up
    rather than silently skipped."""
    if platform == "macos":
        return [agents_home / "Library" / "LaunchAgents" / f"{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}.plist"]
    base = agents_home / ".config" / "systemd" / "user"
    return [base / f"{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}.service", base / f"{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}.timer"]


def remove_deploy_watch(platform: str, agents_home: Path, agents_uid: int,
                        removed: dict[Path, bytes], run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Boot out (macOS) or disable (Linux) and remove the old deploy
    watcher, if it is still there. Idempotent: an install already migrated,
    or one that never carried this ad hoc watcher at all, has nothing at
    these paths and this changes nothing."""
    paths = [p for p in deploy_watch_paths(platform, agents_home) if p.exists()]
    if not paths:
        return
    if platform == "macos":
        run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"gui/{agents_uid}/{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}"],
            capture_output=True, check=False)
    else:
        run(user_manager_argv(AGENTS, agents_uid, "disable", "--now",
            f"{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}.service"), capture_output=True, check=False)
    for path in paths:
        removed[path] = path.read_bytes()
        path.unlink()


def restore_deploy_watch(platform: str, agents_uid: int, removed: dict[Path, bytes],
                         run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Write the old deploy watcher's files back and reload it, for
    rollback. Never raises — the same promise `start_library_units` already
    keeps in `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own rollback path."""
    watch_paths = [p for p in removed if OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL in p.name]
    if not watch_paths:
        return
    for path in watch_paths:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        path.write_bytes(removed[path])
    try:
        if platform == "macos":
            run(["launchctl", "bootstrap", f"gui/{agents_uid}", str(watch_paths[0])],
                capture_output=True, check=False)
        else:
            run(user_manager_argv(AGENTS, agents_uid, "enable", "--now",
                f"{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}.service"), capture_output=True, check=False)
    except OSError:
        pass  # best-effort; the files are back either way


# --- the new root deploy step --------------------------------------------------


def root_deploy_step_loaded(platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> bool:
    """Whether `ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL` is already loaded in the supervisor
    — true only once `bin/orient-deploy` (spec step 4; see the module
    docstring) has been installed and loaded from its own job declaration,
    `jobs/follow-orient-main/orient-deploy.toml`. Never raises: a missing
    `launchctl`/`systemctl` answers False, the same "nothing to find" a real
    refusal path turns into a clear message."""
    if platform == "macos":
        done = run(["launchctl", "print", f"system/{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}"],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        return done.returncode == 0
    done = run(["systemctl", "is-enabled", f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}.timer"],
               capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    return done.returncode == 0


def start_root_deploy_step(platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Start the root deploy step that replaces the old watcher. Refuses,
    naming the missing unit, when it is not loaded — see the module
    docstring's note on `ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL`. This refusal is
    deliberately the one that stops the whole migration before anything is
    rotated."""
    if not root_deploy_step_loaded(platform, run=run):
        raise Refused(
            f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} is not loaded in the supervisor. That is "
            f"bin/orient-deploy's own job, declared in "
            f"jobs/follow-orient-main/orient-deploy.toml. Install and load it first. "
            f"Nothing was migrated.")
    if platform == "macos":
        done = run(["launchctl", "kickstart", "-k", f"system/{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}"],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    else:
        run(["systemctl", "start", f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}.timer"], capture_output=True, check=False)
        done = run(["systemctl", "start", f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}.service"],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} is loaded but could not be started: exited "
            f"{done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing was migrated.")


def stop_root_deploy_step(platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Stop the root deploy step, for rollback. Never raises."""
    try:
        if platform == "macos":
            run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"system/{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}"], capture_output=True, check=False)
        else:
            run(["systemctl", "stop", f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}.service",
                f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}.timer"], capture_output=True, check=False)
    except OSError:
        pass


# --- following Orient's main: placing the deploy step's own record --------------


def follows_orient_main(privileged_prefix: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether root's own `install.json` turns the deploy step on — the
    same check `bin/place-orient`'s own `follows_orient_main` already
    makes before deciding whether to place Orient's code itself or leave
    it to the deploy step. Duplicated here rather than imported, the same
    way every other function this file shares with `place-orient` already
    is."""
    try:
        data = json.loads((privileged_prefix / INSTALL_CONFIG_NAME).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False
    return isinstance(data, dict) and data.get(FOLLOW_ORIENT_MAIN_KEY) is True


def read_deployed_commit(privileged_prefix: Path) -> str | None:
    """The commit root's own deploy step (`bin/orient-deploy`) last
    recorded as deployed, under `{privileged_prefix}/var/orient-deploy/
    deployed` — the same record `bin/orient-deploy`'s own
    `deployed_commit()`/`write_deployed()` read and write. `None` when the
    file is missing or does not hold a commit."""
    try:
        text = (privileged_prefix / ORIENT_DEPLOY_RECORD_REL).read_text(encoding="ascii")
    except OSError:
        return None
    sha = text.strip()
    return sha if SHA.fullmatch(sha) else None


def convivy_orient_repo_name(privileged_prefix: Path) -> str:
    """The convivy-orient repository's own name: install.json's
    `convivy_orient_repo`, falling back to `CONVIVY_ORIENT_REPO` exactly
    where `bin/orient-deploy`'s own `Context.load()` falls back to
    `install-privileged`'s own `DEFAULTS["convivy_orient_repo"]` — when
    install.json does not exist, does not hold a JSON object, or does not
    carry the key. Duplicated from `follows_orient_main`'s own read of
    the same file, rather than read through `install-privileged`'s own
    `load_config`, which refuses outside its own extracted tree at import
    time (see `_install_privileged_for_extract` below): this is read on
    every real run, not only one that follows Orient's main."""
    try:
        data = json.loads((privileged_prefix / INSTALL_CONFIG_NAME).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return CONVIVY_ORIENT_REPO
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return CONVIVY_ORIENT_REPO
    repo = data.get("convivy_orient_repo")
    return repo if isinstance(repo, str) and repo else CONVIVY_ORIENT_REPO


def _install_privileged_for_extract():
    """`bin/install-privileged`'s own `extract()`, loaded only when this
    runs for real — never at import time, including under this file's own
    test suite: install-privileged's own module load refuses unless it
    sits in its own extracted tree (true once this script, too, is run
    from there, the same tree `bin/orient-deploy` runs from; see the
    module docstring), which a test importing this module from an
    arbitrary checkout is not."""
    return _load_sibling("install_privileged_for_migrate_orient_account", "install-privileged")


def extract_deployed_tree(privileged_prefix: Path, sha: str) -> Path:
    """convivy-orient's own tree at `sha`, extracted from root's own bare
    copy exactly as `bin/orient-deploy` extracts a commit —
    `install-privileged`'s own `extract()`, reused here rather than
    duplicated. The bare copy is named from `convivy_orient_repo_name`,
    not the hardcoded `CONVIVY_ORIENT_REPO`, so this agrees with
    `bin/orient-deploy`'s own `Context` about which repository's bare
    copy and commit root's deploy step actually recorded."""
    module = _install_privileged_for_extract()
    repo = convivy_orient_repo_name(privileged_prefix)
    bare = str(privileged_prefix / "src" / f"{repo}.git")
    return Path(module.extract(str(privileged_prefix), bare, repo, sha))


def place_deployed_commit(privileged_prefix: Path, sha: str, orient_name: str, platform: str, *,
                          extract=extract_deployed_tree, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """On an install that follows Orient's main, nothing else puts
    Orient's code under its own account before its first restart:
    `place_orient_code()` leaves it to the deploy step on such an install
    (`bin/place-orient`'s own `follows_orient_main` check), and the deploy
    step itself is not started until Orient's own units are confirmed
    running again, near the end of `migrate()`. This places the commit
    the deploy step already recorded as deployed, with `install-backend
    --from-tree`, in the exact argv shape `bin/place-orient`'s own
    `place_argv()` builds — reused here rather than re-derived. `extract`,
    injectable for a test, defaults to `extract_deployed_tree` above.

    `extract` itself may raise a refusal that is not this script's own:
    the real `extract_deployed_tree` calls into `install-privileged`'s own
    `extract()`, which raises its own `Refused` (`github_gate.Refused`) —
    a class `main()`'s own `except Refused` does not recognise, so it
    would otherwise escape as a traceback after the two tokens are
    already rotated and Orient's state already moved. Caught here by
    class name rather than identity: a test's own injected `extract`
    raises a stand-in for the sibling's class, never the real one, and
    loading the real `install-privileged` here, on every call, would run
    its own location guard against a checkout this script's own test
    suite loads from.

    Loading that sibling (inside `extract_deployed_tree`, through
    `_install_privileged_for_extract`) can also raise `SystemExit`: its
    own `running_copy()` calls `sys.exit(2)` rather than raising
    `Refused` when the extracted tree fails its ownership check.
    `SystemExit` is a `BaseException`, not an `Exception` — `except
    Exception` does not see it, so it is caught here by its own type,
    past the same point of no return, with the same report.
    """
    try:
        tree = extract(privileged_prefix, sha)
    except Refused:
        raise
    except SystemExit as exc:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not extract {sha}'s own tree to place under {orient_name}'s own deploy "
            f"area: install-privileged's own location check refused loading it (exit "
            f"{exc.code}). Orient's own account already holds its rotated tokens and its "
            f"moved state; its own code was not placed, so neither Orient nor "
            f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} may start against it. Place {sha} under {orient_name}'s "
            f"own deploy area by hand once this is resolved, then restart Orient and start "
            f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} yourself. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
    except Exception as exc:
        if type(exc).__name__ != "Refused":
            raise
        raise Refused(
            f"could not extract {sha}'s own tree to place under {orient_name}'s own deploy "
            f"area: {exc} Orient's own account already holds its rotated tokens and its moved "
            f"state; its own code was not placed, so neither Orient nor "
            f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} may start against it. Place {sha} under {orient_name}'s "
            f"own deploy area by hand once this is resolved, then restart Orient and start "
            f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} yourself. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
    interpreter = root_interpreter(platform)
    full, _shown = place_orient.place_argv(tree, sha, None, orient_name, interpreter)
    done = run(full, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not place {sha} under {orient_name}'s own deploy area before its "
            f"restart: install-backend exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")


# --- cloudflared's own old loopback port, checked before a rollback writes back -


def lsof_listener_pid(port: int = ORIENT_OLD_LOOPBACK_PORT, run=subprocess.run) -> int | None:
    """The pid of whatever process `lsof` reports as LISTENing on
    `127.0.0.1:{port}`, read from `lsof -nP -iTCP:{port} -sTCP:LISTEN`'s
    own machine-readable `-F p` (pid-only) output. `None` when nothing is
    listening, or `lsof` itself could not be run."""
    done = run([LSOF, "-nP", f"-iTCP:{port}", "-sTCP:LISTEN", "-F", "p"],
              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return None
    for line in (done.stdout or "").splitlines():
        if line.startswith("p"):
            try:
                return int(line[1:])
            except ValueError:
                continue
    return None


def supervisor_main_pid(label: str, platform: str, run=subprocess.run) -> int | None:
    """The system supervisor's own report of `label`'s main process:
    `launchctl print`'s own `pid = <N>` line on macOS, `systemctl show
    ...MainPID`'s own value on Linux. `None` when the unit is not running,
    is not loaded, or the supervisor cannot be read."""
    if platform == "macos":
        done = run(["launchctl", "print", f"system/{label}"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        if done.returncode != 0:
            return None
        for line in (done.stdout or "").splitlines():
            stripped = line.strip()
            if stripped.startswith("pid = "):
                try:
                    return int(stripped[len("pid = "):])
                except ValueError:
                    return None
        return None
    done = run(["systemctl", "show", f"{label}.service", "-p", "MainPID", "--value"],
              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        pid = int((done.stdout or "").strip())
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return pid or None  # 0 means the unit is not running


def _real_port_connects(port: int) -> bool:
    with socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM) as sock:
        sock.settimeout(2)
        try:
            sock.connect(("127.0.0.1", port))
        except OSError:
            return False
    return True


def confirm_restored_orient_holds_port(platform: str, *, run=subprocess.run,
                                       port_connects=_real_port_connects) -> tuple[bool, str | None]:
    """Whether 127.0.0.1's own old loopback port is held by the restored
    agents' Orient unit's own main process, confirmed two ways: the
    system supervisor's own report of that unit's main pid matches
    `lsof`'s own listener pid, and a connection to the port succeeds.
    Returns `(True, None)` once it is; otherwise `(False, <a description
    of whatever holds the port, or None when nothing does>)` — what a
    rollback's own report names when it leaves the tunnel on the socket.
    Never mistakes an impostor bound to the same port for the restored
    unit: a pid lsof reports that the supervisor's own report does not
    name as that unit's main process is refused, by name, same as no
    listener at all. The two `False` cases are worded differently: a pid
    mismatch names an impostor holding the port instead, while a matching
    pid that still fails to connect names the restored unit itself, not
    yet accepting connections.
    """
    listener_pid = lsof_listener_pid(run=run)
    if listener_pid is None:
        return False, None
    main_pid = supervisor_main_pid(ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS[0], platform, run=run)
    if main_pid is None or main_pid != listener_pid:
        return False, f"pid {listener_pid} holds it instead"
    if not port_connects(ORIENT_OLD_LOOPBACK_PORT):
        return False, (f"the restored Orient (pid {listener_pid}) holds the port but is "
                       f"not yet accepting connections")
    return True, None


def _write_cloudflared_config(config_path: Path, text: str) -> None:
    """Write `text` to `config_path` atomically, preserving the existing
    file's permission bits and ownership. The temporary file is written by
    this process, which runs as root, into a directory the operator owns,
    so every step binds to the fd of the file this call actually created,
    never to the temporary name by path: `O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW` refuses to
    open through a symlink planted there ahead of time, and the chmod/chown
    that follow are `fchmod`/`fchown` on that fd rather than `chmod`/`chown`
    on the path, so neither can be redirected to wherever such a symlink
    points. A regular-file leftover from an earlier, unfinished run is
    removed before the open; anything else at that name is refused rather
    than removed. Shared by `repoint_cloudflared` for its own replacement
    and by `revert_cloudflared` for the rollback."""
    tmp = config_path.with_name(f".{config_path.name}.rollback")
    st = os.stat(config_path)
    try:
        leftover = os.lstat(tmp)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    else:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(leftover.st_mode):
            raise Refused(
                f"refusing {tmp}: it already exists there and is not a plain regular "
                f"file. Nothing further was migrated.")
        os.unlink(tmp)
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW, stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode))
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8", closefd=False) as out:
            out.write(text)
        os.fchmod(fd, stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode))
        os.fchown(fd, st.st_uid, st.st_gid)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    os.replace(tmp, config_path)


def revert_cloudflared(config_path: Path, original_text: str, operator: str, operator_uid: int,
                       platform: str, label: str, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Write `original_text` — the bytes `repoint_cloudflared` found
    before this run changed them — back to `config_path`, atomically, and
    restart `cloudflared`. Called only once
    `confirm_restored_orient_holds_port` has confirmed the restored,
    agents'-own Orient truly holds the old loopback port."""
    _write_cloudflared_config(config_path, original_text)
    restart_cloudflared(operator, operator_uid, platform, label, run=run)


# --- the symlink-safe tree copy, duplicated from bin/migrate-library-account ---


def _open_dir(parent_fd: int, name: str, shown: str) -> int:
    try:
        return os.open(name, DIR_OPEN, dir_fd=parent_fd)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: {exc.strerror}. Nothing was migrated.") from exc


def _read_hashing(fin, fout=None, chunk_size: int = 1 << 20) -> tuple[int, str]:
    import hashlib
    hasher = hashlib.sha256()
    size = 0
    while True:
        chunk = fin.read(chunk_size)
        if not chunk:
            break
        hasher.update(chunk)
        if fout is not None:
            fout.write(chunk)
        size += len(chunk)
    return size, hasher.hexdigest()


def copy_tree(src_fd: int, dst_fd: int, shown: str, uid: int, gid: int,
              manifest: dict[str, tuple[int, str]] | None = None, rel: str = "") -> None:
    """Copy every entry under `src_fd` into `dst_fd`, owned by `(uid, gid)`,
    private permission bits only. Never follows a symlink, and refuses any
    special file. Duplicated from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own
    version of this function; see that file's own docstring for why."""
    for name in sorted(os.listdir(src_fd)):
        info = os.stat(name, dir_fd=src_fd, follow_symlinks=False)
        entry_shown = f"{shown}/{name}"
        entry_rel = f"{rel}/{name}" if rel else name
        if stat.S_ISDIR(info.st_mode):
            os.mkdir(name, 0o700, dir_fd=dst_fd)
            child_dst = _open_dir(dst_fd, name, entry_shown)
            try:
                os.fchown(child_dst, uid, gid)
                child_src = _open_dir(src_fd, name, entry_shown)
                try:
                    copy_tree(child_src, child_dst, entry_shown, uid, gid, manifest, entry_rel)
                finally:
                    os.close(child_src)
            finally:
                os.close(child_dst)
        elif stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
            in_fd = os.open(name, FILE_OPEN, dir_fd=src_fd)
            try:
                out_fd = os.open(name, NEW_FILE, 0o600, dir_fd=dst_fd)
                try:
                    with os.fdopen(in_fd, "rb", closefd=False) as fin, \
                            os.fdopen(out_fd, "wb", closefd=False) as fout:
                        size, digest = _read_hashing(fin, fout)
                    os.fchown(out_fd, uid, gid)
                finally:
                    os.close(out_fd)
            finally:
                os.close(in_fd)
            if manifest is not None:
                manifest[entry_rel] = (size, digest)
        else:
            kind = "a symlink" if stat.S_ISLNK(info.st_mode) else "neither a regular file nor a directory"
            raise Refused(
                f"refusing {entry_shown}: it is {kind}, which this will not follow or copy. "
                f"Nothing was migrated.")


def hash_tree(root: Path) -> dict[str, tuple[int, str]]:
    """Duplicated from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of this
    function."""
    listing: dict[str, tuple[int, str]] = {}
    for dirpath, _dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root, followlinks=False):
        for name in filenames:
            path = Path(dirpath, name)
            with open(path, "rb") as f:
                size, digest = _read_hashing(f)
            listing[str(path.relative_to(root))] = (size, digest)
    return listing


def verify_tree_copy(manifest: dict[str, tuple[int, str]], dst: Path) -> None:
    """Duplicated from `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own version of this
    function."""
    after = hash_tree(dst)
    if manifest != after:
        missing = sorted(set(manifest) - set(after))
        wrong = sorted(k for k in set(manifest) & set(after) if manifest[k] != after[k])
        extra = sorted(set(after) - set(manifest))
        raise Refused(
            f"the copy at {dst} does not match what was read from its source: missing={missing[:5]} "
            f"wrong={wrong[:5]} unexpected={extra[:5]}. Nothing was migrated.")


# --- flat file moves, for .orient-control's and the config root's own remainder -


def _copied_so_far(copied: list[str]) -> str:
    """The clause every `copy_flat_files` refusal below appends: this call
    runs only after the two Library tokens are already rotated (the point
    of no return — see `migrate()`'s own call site), so none of its own
    refusals may claim nothing was migrated, and whatever it already
    copied before failing has to be named rather than lost in a generic
    message."""
    return f" Already copied this call: {', '.join(copied)}." if copied else " Nothing copied this call yet."


def copy_flat_files(src_dir: Path, dst_dir: Path, names: tuple[str, ...], uid: int, gid: int,
                    owner_uid: int, copied: list[str]) -> None:
    """Copy every one of `names` directly under `src_dir`, that is actually
    there, into `dst_dir`, owned by `(uid, gid)`, appending each copied name
    to `copied`. Never follows a symlink, and refuses a special file or a
    name already present at the destination — the same discipline
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own `copy_credentials` already holds,
    generalised past `is_known_credential`'s own name list to whatever
    subset of `names` this call is given. Called only after the two
    Library tokens are already rotated, so every refusal here names what
    this call already copied instead of claiming nothing was migrated."""
    if not src_dir.is_dir():
        return
    src_fd = os.open(str(src_dir), DIR_OPEN)
    try:
        if os.fstat(src_fd).st_uid != owner_uid:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {src_dir}: it is not owned by {AGENTS}.{_copied_so_far(copied)}")
        dst_dir.mkdir(mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        dst_fd = os.open(str(dst_dir), DIR_OPEN)
        try:
            os.fchown(dst_fd, uid, gid)
            present = set(os.listdir(src_fd))
            for name in sorted(names):
                if name not in present:
                    continue
                info = os.stat(name, dir_fd=src_fd, follow_symlinks=False)
                if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
                    raise Refused(
                        f"refusing {src_dir}/{name}: it is not a plain regular file."
                        f"{_copied_so_far(copied)}")
                in_fd = os.open(name, FILE_OPEN, dir_fd=src_fd)
                try:
                    try:
                        out_fd = os.open(name, NEW_FILE, 0o600, dir_fd=dst_fd)
                    except FileExistsError as exc:
                        raise Refused(
                            f"refusing {dst_dir}/{name}: it already exists there. This looks "
                            f"like a previous migration that did not finish cleanly. Resolve "
                            f"by hand.{_copied_so_far(copied)}") from exc
                    try:
                        with os.fdopen(in_fd, "rb", closefd=False) as fin, \
                                os.fdopen(out_fd, "wb", closefd=False) as fout:
                            shutil.copyfileobj(fin, fout)
                        os.fchown(out_fd, uid, gid)
                    finally:
                        os.close(out_fd)
                finally:
                    os.close(in_fd)
                copied.append(name)
        finally:
            os.close(dst_fd)
    finally:
        os.close(src_fd)


def remaining_agents_orient_files(agents_home: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Which of the files the module docstring's own table gives Orient —
    both Library tokens, `.orient-control`'s other names, and the config
    root's own five — are still present under `agents`' own home. Named
    for the report a failure after the token rotation gives the operator:
    each is deleted by hand, once Orient's own copy is confirmed, so
    commitment 5 (`agents` holds no human-reserved capability) holds on
    this install too."""
    found = []
    agents_oc = agents_orient_control(agents_home)
    for name in (*ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES, *ORIENT_CONTROL_OTHER_NAMES):
        if os.path.lexists(agents_oc / name):
            found.append(str(agents_oc / name))
    config_root = agents_home / ORIENT_CONFIG_REL
    for name in ORIENT_CONFIG_ROOT_NAMES:
        if os.path.lexists(config_root / name):
            found.append(str(config_root / name))
    return found


def move_sibling_logs(agents_home: Path, orient_home: Path, agents_uid: int,
                      orient_ids: tuple[int, int]) -> list[str]:
    """Copy every one of `ORIENT_SIBLING_LOGS` that is actually there from
    `{agents_home}/.local/state` to the same place under Orient's own
    account, and remove the original on success. Best-effort: called only
    after Orient's state is already promoted, so a failure here is reported
    by the caller rather than rolled back, the same way
    `bin/migrate-library-account`'s own `move_sibling_log` is. Returns the
    names it moved."""
    moved: list[str] = []
    src_dir = agents_home / STATE_REL
    dst_dir = orient_home / STATE_REL
    for name in ORIENT_SIBLING_LOGS:
        src = src_dir / name
        try:
            in_fd = os.open(str(src), FILE_OPEN)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        try:
            info = os.fstat(in_fd)
            if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode) or info.st_uid != agents_uid:
                os.close(in_fd)
                continue
            mkdir_owned(dst_dir, *orient_ids)
            dst = dst_dir / name
            if os.path.lexists(dst):
                os.close(in_fd)
                continue
            out_fd = os.open(str(dst), NEW_FILE, 0o600)
            try:
                with os.fdopen(in_fd, "rb", closefd=False) as fin, \
                        os.fdopen(out_fd, "wb", closefd=False) as fout:
                    shutil.copyfileobj(fin, fout)
                os.fchown(out_fd, *orient_ids)
            finally:
                os.close(out_fd)
        finally:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.close(in_fd)
        os.remove(src)
        moved.append(name)
    return moved


# --- credential rotation --------------------------------------------------------


def library_deploy_dir(library_home: Path) -> Path:
    return library_home / ".local" / "deploy" / "library"


def mint_credential_argv(library_home: Path, *extra: str) -> list[str]:
    deploy = library_deploy_dir(library_home)
    return [str(deploy / ".venv" / "bin" / "python"), str(deploy / "mint_credential.py"), *extra]


def mint_credential_env(library_home: Path) -> dict[str, str]:
    """The environment `mint_credential.py` runs under — `HOME` naming the
    Library's own account, the same way `jobs/library.toml`'s own
    `[environment] HOME = "{home}"` does for every `prepare` entry that
    calls it. Called inside a child `run_as_account` has already dropped to
    that account's own uid and gid, which does not by itself change `HOME`
    — the fork inherits root's own environment — so every subprocess call
    into `mint_credential.py` below passes this explicitly rather than
    relying on inherited state.
    """
    return {"HOME": str(library_home), "PATH": "/usr/bin:/bin"}


def library_role_withholds(library_home: Path, grant: str, run=subprocess.run) -> list[str]:
    """Every capability the Library's own `agent` role grants, except
    `grant` itself — computed by running the Library's own interpreter
    against the Library's own tree, the identical `identity.ROLE_CAPS
    ["agent"]` computation `jobs/library.toml`'s own mint of
    `capture-token` and `kb-person-save-token` runs at install time —
    rather than a tuple written out here, which a later release adding a
    capability to that role would silently grant instead of withhold.
    Runs with the Library's own deploy directory as the working directory,
    the same `working-directory` `jobs/library.toml` names for every
    `prepare` step, so `import identity` resolves the Library's own module
    rather than failing to find it or finding some other one on this
    process's own path. Refuses, naming `grant`, when the computation
    fails or yields nothing to withhold — an empty list would mint a
    credential holding every agent capability, the opposite of what
    either mint calling this is for.
    """
    deploy = library_deploy_dir(library_home)
    # `identity` imports `store`, whose module top level refuses to load
    # without LIBRARY_DB or ORIENT_CONTROL_DB. The job's own mints run under
    # the job's [environment], which sets LIBRARY_DB; this runs under
    # `mint_credential_env`, which does not. So the script sets the same
    # never-opened placeholder `mint_credential.py` itself sets before its
    # own imports, with `setdefault`, and reads no database.
    script = ("import os\n"
              "os.environ.setdefault('LIBRARY_DB', '/nonexistent/role-withholds-placeholder.db')\n"
              "import identity, sys\n"
             f"sys.stdout.write(','.join(sorted(c for c in identity.ROLE_CAPS['agent'] if c != {grant!r})))")
    argv = [str(deploy / ".venv" / "bin" / "python"), "-c", script]
    done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
              cwd=str(deploy), env=mint_credential_env(library_home))
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not compute --withholds for {grant} from the Library's own identity module: "
            f"exited {done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing was minted.")
    withholds = sorted({c for c in done.stdout.strip().split(",") if c})
    if not withholds:
        raise Refused(
            f"computing --withholds for {grant} from the Library's own identity module yielded "
            f"nothing to withhold. Refusing to mint a credential holding every agent capability. "
            f"Nothing was minted.")
    return withholds


def tokens_file_path(library_home: Path, store_file: str) -> Path:
    """Where `store_file`'s own token store (`"agents"` or `"clients"`)
    sits on disk, under the Library account's own state directory."""
    return library_home / STATE_REL / "library" / ORIENT_TOKEN_STORE_FILENAMES[store_file]


# Every field `mint_credential.py --replace` can rebuild an entry from —
# every one of its own non-secret, non-timestamp flags (`--kind`,
# `--products`, `--grants`, `--holds-reserved`, `--withholds`, `--profile`,
# `--crew`, `--entity`, `--all-entities`). Restricting a read entry to
# exactly this set, rather than keeping everything the JSON holds, is what
# keeps the secret — the store's own key for the entry, never a field
# inside it — and `created_at` out of both the `--replace` argv and the
# before/after comparison, with no need to name either by its own key.
ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS = (
    "kind", "products", "grants", "holds_reserved", "withholds",
    "profile", "crew", "entity", "all_entities",
)


def read_store_entry(tokens_file: Path, name: str, *, required: bool = True) -> dict | None:
    """`name`'s own entry in `tokens_file`, a JSON object keyed by each
    entry's own secret, with the entry found by its `name` field rather than
    by the key. Returns the entry's value restricted to
    `ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS`, so neither the secret that keys it nor
    `created_at` is ever read into it. Refuses, naming `tokens_file`, when
    it cannot be read or parsed as a JSON object, or when `name` is carried
    by more than one entry — the same ambiguity `mint_credential.py`'s own
    `--revoke` refuses. Also refuses, naming the field, when the entry
    carries a field outside `ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS` and outside `name`
    and `created_at` — a field `mint_credential.py --replace` cannot be
    told to carry forward, rather than one this script could silently drop.
    When `name` is carried by no entry, refuses the same way unless
    `required` is False, in which case this returns None — an install that
    predates the credential's own mint carries no such entry at all.
    """
    try:
        text = tokens_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not read {tokens_file}: {exc}. Nothing was migrated.") from exc
    try:
        store = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"{tokens_file} does not hold valid JSON: {exc}. Nothing was migrated.") from exc
    if not isinstance(store, dict):
        raise Refused(f"{tokens_file} does not hold a JSON object. Nothing was migrated.")
    matches = [entry for entry in store.values()
              if isinstance(entry, dict) and entry.get("name") == name]
    if not matches and not required:
        return None
    if len(matches) != 1:
        raise Refused(
            f"{name} is carried by {len(matches)} entries in {tokens_file}, not exactly one. "
            f"Nothing was migrated.")
    entry = matches[0]
    unknown = sorted(set(entry) - set(ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS) - {"name", "created_at"})
    if unknown:
        raise Refused(
            f"{name}'s own entry in {tokens_file} carries {', '.join(unknown)}, outside "
            f"ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS and neither name nor created_at. Refusing to rotate "
            f"past a field this script does not know to carry forward. Nothing was migrated.")
    return {k: entry[k] for k in ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS if k in entry}


def replace_argv_for(library_home: Path, store_file: str, tokens_file: Path, name: str,
                     out_path: Path, fields: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The full `mint_credential.py --replace` command line that rebuilds
    `name`'s entry in `store_file` from `fields` (`read_store_entry`'s own
    result) — every one of `ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS` the entry actually
    carries, each passed back as `--<key with dashes for underscores>
    <value>`, a list value joined with commas, and an empty list or empty
    string passed as `--<flag>=` (the same empty-value shape
    `jobs/library.toml`'s own `--holds-reserved=` already uses) so that a
    withheld grant is reasserted as withheld rather than silently dropped.
    A null value is skipped: `mint_credential.py` reads its absence as the
    field's own default. `--tokens-file` is always passed explicitly, the
    same way `bin/deliver-library-credentials` always passes it to
    `mint-crew-ops-credentials`, rather than relying on `mint_credential.py`'s
    own environment-variable default.
    """
    argv = mint_credential_argv(library_home, "--file", store_file, "--name", name,
                                "--tokens-file", str(tokens_file), "--replace", "--out", str(out_path))
    for key, value in fields.items():
        if value is None:
            continue
        flag = "--" + key.replace("_", "-")
        rendered = ",".join(str(v) for v in value) if isinstance(value, list) else str(value)
        if rendered == "":
            argv.append(f"{flag}=")  # an empty list/string, reasserted as withheld, not dropped
        else:
            argv += [flag, rendered]
    return argv


def compare_entries(before: dict, after: dict, name: str, store_file: str) -> None:
    """Refuse unless `before` and `after` — each already restricted to
    `ORIENT_TOKEN_ENTRY_FIELDS` by `read_store_entry`, so neither carries
    the secret that keys the entry or `created_at` — agree on every field,
    the verification this design requires after every `--replace`."""
    if before != after:
        raise Refused(
            f"{name}'s own fields in the {store_file} store changed when it was replaced: "
            f"before={before} after={after}. Refusing — this would have dropped or changed a "
            f"grant. Nothing further was migrated.")


def rotate_orient_token(library_home: Path, library_ids: tuple[int, int], store_file: str,
                        out_path: Path, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Rotate one of Orient's two Library tokens with `--replace`, reading
    its own entry directly from the token store first and refusing unless
    it agrees with what comes back afterward. Runs entirely as the
    Library's own account, by `run_as_account`, the same boundary every
    other read or write of either account's own files already holds in
    this file and in `bin/migrate-library-account`."""
    tokens_file = tokens_file_path(library_home, store_file)

    def _rotate() -> dict:
        before = read_store_entry(tokens_file, ORIENT_MINT_NAME)
        argv = replace_argv_for(library_home, store_file, tokens_file, ORIENT_MINT_NAME, out_path, before)
        done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=mint_credential_env(library_home))
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(
                f"could not rotate {ORIENT_MINT_NAME}'s token in the {store_file} store: "
                f"mint_credential.py --replace exited {done.returncode}: "
                f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")
        after = read_store_entry(tokens_file, ORIENT_MINT_NAME)
        compare_entries(before, after, ORIENT_MINT_NAME, store_file)
        return after

    run_as_account(*library_ids, _rotate)


def capture_token_mint_name() -> str:
    """The name `capture-token` is minted under: `CAPTURE_TOKEN_MINT_PREFIX`
    plus this machine's own short hostname, lowercased — the same name a
    fresh install's `jobs/library.toml` own `prepare` mint computes (there,
    via `socket.gethostname()` through `{python} -c`), so the two paths that
    can mint this credential never disagree about its name on one machine.
    Per-machine, rather than the bare prefix alone, so a later
    `mint_credential.py --revoke` of it is unambiguous."""
    return CAPTURE_TOKEN_MINT_PREFIX + socket.gethostname().split(".")[0].lower()


def mint_capture_token(library_home: Path, library_ids: tuple[int, int],
                       run=subprocess.run, withholds: list[str] | None = None) -> bool:
    """Mint Orient's own narrow signal credential into the Library account's
    own `.orient-control/capture-token`, unless it is already there —
    whether from an earlier run of this function or from a fresh install's
    `jobs/library.toml` own `prepare`, which mints into the exact same path
    under its own `creates` guard. Runs entirely as the Library's own
    account, by `run_as_account`, the same boundary `rotate_orient_token`
    already holds. Passes `--tokens-file` explicitly, the same way
    `replace_argv_for` does, because `mint_credential.py` refuses to guess
    which file holds the agents token. Returns whether it minted anything.
    """
    out_path = library_home / ORIENT_CONTROL / CAPTURE_TOKEN_NAME

    def _mint() -> bool:
        if out_path.exists():
            return False
        computed = withholds if withholds is not None else \
            library_role_withholds(library_home, CAPTURE_TOKEN_GRANT, run=run)
        argv = mint_credential_argv(
            library_home, "--file", "agents", "--name", capture_token_mint_name(),
            "--kind", "agent", "--products", "convivy-orient",
            "--withholds", ",".join(computed),
            "--tokens-file", str(tokens_file_path(library_home, "agents")),
            "--hashed", "--out", str(out_path))
        done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=mint_credential_env(library_home))
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(
                f"could not mint Orient's own {CAPTURE_TOKEN_NAME}: mint_credential.py exited "
                f"{done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")
        return True

    return run_as_account(*library_ids, _mint)


def mint_kb_person_save_token(library_home: Path, library_ids: tuple[int, int],
                              run=subprocess.run, withholds: list[str] | None = None) -> bool:
    """Mint `kb-person-save-token` into the Library account's own
    `.orient-control/kb-person-save-token`, unless it is already there —
    whether from an earlier run of this function or from a fresh install's
    `jobs/library.toml` own `prepare`, which mints into the exact same path
    under its own `creates` guard. Runs entirely as the Library's own
    account, by `run_as_account`, the same boundary `mint_capture_token`
    already holds. The caller (`migrate()`) only calls this when `agents`
    itself still has no copy — see `KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME`'s own comment
    for why this account may already hold one `deliver_to_orient` below has
    not yet delivered. Returns whether it minted anything.
    """
    out_path = library_home / ORIENT_CONTROL / KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME

    def _mint() -> bool:
        if out_path.exists():
            return False
        computed = withholds if withholds is not None else \
            library_role_withholds(library_home, KB_PERSON_SAVE_GRANT, run=run)
        argv = mint_credential_argv(
            library_home, "--file", "agents", "--name", "kb-person-save",
            "--grants", KB_PERSON_SAVE_GRANT,
            "--withholds", ",".join(computed),
            "--tokens-file", str(tokens_file_path(library_home, "agents")),
            "--out", str(out_path))
        done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=mint_credential_env(library_home))
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(
                f"could not mint {KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME}: mint_credential.py exited "
                f"{done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")
        return True

    return run_as_account(*library_ids, _mint)


def remint_kb_person_save_crew(library_home: Path, library_ids: tuple[int, int],
                               run=subprocess.run) -> bool:
    """Re-mint `kb-person-save` with no crew, with `mint_credential.py
    --replace`, when the Library's own `agents` store already carries it
    under a crew, the shape an earlier mint wrote. A crew the install's
    project registry has no row for makes the token authenticate nobody
    (see `KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME`'s own comment), so this drops the crew
    in place rather than leaving it for `mint_kb_person_save_token`, which
    only ever mints where there is none. Reads the entry's own fields first
    and passes them all back unchanged but for `crew`, which `--replace`
    then omits, and refuses, by `compare_entries`, unless they still agree
    afterward. Returns False, changing nothing, when the store carries no
    `kb-person-save` entry at all or carries one with no crew. Runs
    entirely as the Library's own account, the same boundary
    `rotate_orient_token` already holds.
    """
    tokens_file = tokens_file_path(library_home, "agents")
    out_path = library_home / ORIENT_CONTROL / KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME

    def _remint() -> bool:
        before = read_store_entry(tokens_file, "kb-person-save", required=False)
        if before is None or before.get("crew") is None:
            return False
        wanted = {k: v for k, v in before.items() if k != "crew"}
        argv = replace_argv_for(library_home, "agents", tokens_file, "kb-person-save", out_path, wanted)
        done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, env=mint_credential_env(library_home))
        if done.returncode != 0:
            raise Refused(
                f"could not re-mint kb-person-save with no crew: "
                f"mint_credential.py --replace exited {done.returncode}: "
                f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")
        after = read_store_entry(tokens_file, "kb-person-save")
        compare_entries(wanted, after, "kb-person-save", "agents")
        return True

    return run_as_account(*library_ids, _remint)


def deliver_to_orient(run=subprocess.run, install_json: str | None = None) -> None:
    """Deliver the rotated tokens and `capture-token` to Orient's own account,
    and whatever `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` names — `kb-person-save-token`
    included — to `agents`, with `bin/deliver-library-credentials`, the same
    copy this tool ships beside. That tool already routes all three of
    `ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` to Orient's account rather than to `agents`,
    and only overwrites a name whose bytes differ from what the destination
    already holds, so this is exactly what carries the rotated values, and
    `capture-token` if `mint_capture_token` minted it, across — and, in the
    same call, `kb-person-save-token` if `mint_kb_person_save_token` minted
    it."""
    tool = REPO / "bin" / "deliver-library-credentials"
    platform = "macos" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux"
    interpreter = root_interpreter(platform)
    argv = [interpreter, str(tool)]
    if install_json:
        argv += ["--install-json", install_json]
    done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not deliver the rotated tokens to Orient's own account: "
            f"deliver-library-credentials exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")


# --- the statement key and its verifier entry -----------------------------------


def run_orient_make_statement_key(orient_home: Path, orient_ids: tuple[int, int], tree: Path,
                                  python: Path, run=subprocess.run) -> str:
    """Run `bin/orient-make-statement-key` as Orient's own account, from
    the tree root `place-orient` just placed — never `agents`' own
    checkout, and never as the operator — and return the one line of
    base64 it prints: the standard base64 of the public key's DER
    SubjectPublicKeyInfo. Idempotent in the sense the spec gives it: a
    second run refuses because the key file already exists, which this
    treats as "already done" rather than a failure, returning None for the
    caller to leave `kb-verifier.json` as it already is.

    `sudo` clears the environment, so `ORIENT_CONFIG_ROOT` and
    `ORIENT_STATE_ROOT` are passed explicitly via `env`, matching the
    values `jobs/orient.toml`'s `[environment]` gives Orient's own
    service. Without them, Orient's `paths._resolve_root` falls back to
    the tree's own directory, and the key lands outside the config root
    the service reads, or fails outright if that directory isn't
    writable.
    """
    script = tree / "bin" / "orient-make-statement-key"
    uid, gid = orient_ids
    config_root = orient_home / ORIENT_CONFIG_REL
    argv = [SUDO, "-u", orient_account_name(), "-H", "env",
            f"ORIENT_CONFIG_ROOT={config_root}", f"ORIENT_STATE_ROOT={config_root}",
            str(python), str(script)]
    done = run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        if "exists" in (done.stderr or "").lower():
            return ""
        raise Refused(
            f"orient-make-statement-key exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")
    line = (done.stdout or "").strip()
    if not line or "\n" in line:
        raise Refused(
            f"orient-make-statement-key printed something other than one line: {done.stdout!r}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    return line


_ALREADY_ROTATED = ("The two Library tokens were already rotated; the statement key was "
                    "not placed.")


def _open_or_create_operator_dir(parent_fd: int, name: str, shown: Path,
                                 operator_ids: tuple[int, int]) -> int:
    """Open `name` under `parent_fd`, relative to that `fd` and never by
    path, refusing it — by whatever `OSError` the kernel gives `DIR_OPEN`'s
    own `O_NOFOLLOW` for a symlink or anything but a directory — rather
    than resolving it. Creates it — mode 0700, owned by `operator_ids` —
    when it is not there yet. Called once for every directory on the walk
    down from the operator's home to the verifier's own `kb-verifier.json`,
    the same way `copy_tree`'s own walk uses `_open_dir`: a symlink planted
    at any level of that walk, not only at the verifier's own name, must
    not steer where root ends up writing, `chmod`-ing or `chown`-ing."""
    created = False
    try:
        fd = os.open(name, DIR_OPEN, dir_fd=parent_fd)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        try:
            os.mkdir(name, 0o700, dir_fd=parent_fd)
            created = True
        except FileExistsError:
            pass
        except OSError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"could not create {shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
        try:
            fd = os.open(name, DIR_OPEN, dir_fd=parent_fd)
        except OSError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    if created:
        try:
            os.fchown(fd, *operator_ids)
        except OSError as exc:
            os.close(fd)
            raise Refused(f"could not set the owner of {shown}: {exc.strerror}. "
                          f"{_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    return fd


def _write_statement_key(dir_fd: int, leaf: str, dir_shown: Path,
                         operator_ids: tuple[int, int], pubkey_line: str) -> None:
    """Write `pubkey_line` into `leaf`, relative to `dir_fd` and never by
    path, the same symlink-refusing, `OSError`-to-`Refused` discipline
    `place_statement_key_in_verifier`'s own docstring describes. Leaves a
    temporary file behind, named `.<leaf>.migrating`, only when a step
    after it was opened fails."""
    path_shown = dir_shown / leaf
    tmp_name = f".{leaf}.migrating"
    tmp_shown = dir_shown / tmp_name
    try:
        info = os.lstat(leaf, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        info = None
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path_shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    if info is not None and stat.S_ISLNK(info.st_mode):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path_shown}: it is a symlink. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}")
    data: dict = {}
    if info is not None and stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
        try:
            fd_r = os.open(leaf, FILE_OPEN, dir_fd=dir_fd)
            try:
                with os.fdopen(fd_r, "r", closefd=False, encoding="utf-8") as fin:
                    text = fin.read()
            finally:
                os.close(fd_r)
        except OSError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"could not read {path_shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
        try:
            data = json.loads(text)
        except ValueError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"{path_shown} does not hold valid JSON: {exc}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            raise Refused(f"{path_shown} does not hold a JSON object. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}")
    data["orient_statement_key"] = pubkey_line

    try:
        tmp_info = os.lstat(tmp_name, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        tmp_info = None
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {tmp_shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    if tmp_info is not None:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(tmp_info.st_mode):
            raise Refused(
                f"refusing {tmp_shown}: it exists and is not a plain regular file. "
                f"{_ALREADY_ROTATED}")
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp_name, dir_fd=dir_fd)
        except OSError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"could not remove the leftover {tmp_shown}: {exc.strerror}. "
                          f"{_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc

    try:
        fd = os.open(tmp_name, NEW_FILE, 0o600, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    except FileExistsError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {tmp_shown}: it already exists. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not create {tmp_shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    try:
        try:
            with os.fdopen(fd, "w", closefd=False, encoding="utf-8") as out:
                out.write(json.dumps(data, indent=2) + "\n")
            os.fchmod(fd, 0o600)
            os.fchown(fd, *operator_ids)
            written = os.fstat(fd)
            os.replace(tmp_name, leaf, src_dir_fd=dir_fd, dst_dir_fd=dir_fd)
        except OSError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"could not place {path_shown}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    except Refused:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp_name, dir_fd=dir_fd)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise
    finally:
        os.close(fd)

    try:
        after = os.lstat(leaf, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not confirm {path_shown} after placing it: {exc.strerror}. "
                      f"The two Library tokens were already rotated.") from exc
    if not stat.S_ISREG(after.st_mode) or (after.st_dev, after.st_ino) != (written.st_dev, written.st_ino):
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path_shown}: right after placing it, it is not the file this call "
            f"just wrote. The two Library tokens were already rotated.")


def place_statement_key_in_verifier(operator_home: Path, operator_ids: tuple[int, int],
                                    pubkey_line: str) -> None:
    """Place `pubkey_line` unchanged under `orient_statement_key` in the
    operator's own `kb-verifier.json`, leaving any `persons` entry already
    there untouched. Creates the file, mode 0600, owned by the operator,
    when it is not there yet: a new file holding `persons` and
    `orient_statement_key`.

    Runs as root, called only after the two Library tokens are already
    rotated — the point of no return — in a home directory the operator
    owns end to end, but whose contents, before this call ever runs, an
    attacker able to write there beforehand controls. Every directory from
    `operator_home` down to the verifier's own `kb-verifier.json` is
    therefore opened component by component, relative to its own parent's
    `fd` (`_open_or_create_operator_dir`, the same `DIR_OPEN` primitive
    `copy_tree`'s own walk uses), never resolved by path: a symlink planted
    at any level of that walk — not only at the verifier's own name — is
    refused rather than followed, and a missing directory is created, mode
    0700 and owned by the operator, rather than left to a path-based
    `mkdir(parents=True)` that would walk through a symlink the same way a
    plain `open` would. The verifier itself and the fixed-name temporary
    file this writes through (`_write_statement_key`) are opened the same
    way, relative to that pinned directory: both are refused by `os.lstat`
    when either is a symlink, never by a path operation that would follow
    one. The temporary file is opened with `O_EXCL`, same as `NEW_FILE`
    everywhere else in this script, so a leftover there is removed first,
    and only when it is a plain regular file.

    The temporary file's own descriptor stays open through the rename:
    right after `os.replace`, this `lstat`s the verifier's own name again
    and refuses it unless it is a regular file sharing that descriptor's
    own `(st_dev, st_ino)` — the rename landed what this call just wrote,
    not something swapped into the temporary name in the gap between
    writing it and renaming it over. Any other `OSError` along this walk —
    the traversal, the lstats, the read, the write, `fchmod`, `fchown` or
    the rename itself — becomes a `Refused` naming the step, never a raw
    traceback, since this runs past the point of no return; the temporary
    file is removed on any failure that leaves it behind.
    """
    if not pubkey_line:
        return  # the key already existed; leave the verifier as it is
    dir_names = KB_VERIFIER_REL.parts[:-1]
    leaf = KB_VERIFIER_REL.parts[-1]
    try:
        dir_fd = os.open(str(operator_home), DIR_OPEN)
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"refusing {operator_home}: {exc.strerror}. {_ALREADY_ROTATED}") from exc
    try:
        shown = operator_home
        for name in dir_names:
            shown = shown / name
            next_fd = _open_or_create_operator_dir(dir_fd, name, shown, operator_ids)
            os.close(dir_fd)
            dir_fd = next_fd
        _write_statement_key(dir_fd, leaf, shown, operator_ids, pubkey_line)
    finally:
        os.close(dir_fd)


# --- the Library's own person-save inputs ---------------------------------------


def place_library_person_save_inputs(library_home: Path, library_ids: tuple[int, int],
                                     root_ca: str, app_key: str, app_config: str,
                                     persons: list[str] | None, key: str | None,
                                     run_as=run_as_account) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Place the Library's seven person-save inputs under
    `{library_home}/.library/person-save/`, through a child that has dropped
    to the Library account's own uid and gid — delivery writes into another
    account, so root opens nothing in that home. Reuses `bin/place-person-save`'s
    own `place_entries()` (loaded as `place_person_save` above) rather than a
    second copy.

    `key` is the line this migration already captured from
    `bin/orient-make-statement-key`'s own stdout, held in memory by the
    caller — never read back from `kb-verifier.json` or any other file, since
    whoever could write that file before the copy would choose the key the
    Library trusts from then on. `persons` is the operator's own list, from
    `--persons`, the same way `--root-ca`, `--app-key` and `--app-config` are
    the operator's own files. `key=None` places the six entries that need no
    Orient key, the same as a fresh install's own placement before Orient has
    made one.
    """
    d = library_home / ".library" / "person-save"
    # Read as root, before the drop: the Library account cannot read the
    # operator's home, where these files usually are. A macOS rehearsal
    # refused with "Permission denied" on the operator's --root-ca.
    try:
        root_ca = place_person_save.preread(root_ca, "--root-ca")
        app_key = place_person_save.preread(app_key, "--app-key")
        app_config = place_person_save.preread(app_config, "--app-config")
    except place_person_save.Refused as exc:
        raise Refused(str(exc)) from exc
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not read {exc.filename}: {exc.strerror}. Nothing was placed.") from exc

    def _place():
        gate = place_person_save.load_gate()
        gh = gate.GitHub()
        return place_person_save.place_entries(
            d, root_ca=root_ca, key=key, persons=persons,
            app_key=app_key, app_config=app_config, gate=gate, gh=gh)

    return run_as(*library_ids, _place)


# --- the shared agent-platform deploy area, read by the document service -------

# Where the Bosun's `install-agent-platform-shared` builds the area, under
# `agents`' own home — the account the document service runs as, which finds
# the area under its own home and never on `PATH`.
AGENT_PLATFORM_SHARED_REL = Path(".local", "deploy", "agent-platform-shared")


def install_agent_platform_shared(agent_platform_tree: Path, agents_home: Path, platform: str,
                                  run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Deploy-pin the shared agent-platform area the document service runs
    `agentctl` from, with the Bosun's `bin/install-agent-platform-shared`, run
    as `agents` — before the document service is installed, below, which
    needs the area to exist the moment it starts taking saves. Reads root's
    own bare copy of agent-platform at the commit of the placed tree the rest
    of this migration already uses, the same copy and the same commit
    `install_document_service` below reads `install-kb-document-api` from.

    `--allow-non-main` is required: root's own record is rarely the literal
    ref `main`, and the installer's own rule 6 refuses anything else without
    it. `--deploy-dir` is passed explicitly, naming `agents`' own area, so a
    non-main ref into the DEFAULT production path — which that installer's
    own rule 6 additionally refuses without naming the path explicitly —
    never happens by omission. The Bosun owns that script; this only calls
    it, the same split `install_document_service` already draws for
    `install-kb-document-api`.

    Refuses outright on Linux, the same as `install_document_service`: the
    document service this deploys for has no Linux shape designed yet.
    """
    if platform != "macos":
        raise Refused(
            "install_agent_platform_shared: refusing on Linux — the document service this "
            "deploys for has no Linux shape designed yet. Nothing further was migrated.")
    tool = agent_platform_tree / "bin" / "install-agent-platform-shared"
    if not tool.is_file():
        raise Refused(
            f"{tool} does not exist in the placed agent-platform tree. Nothing further was "
            f"migrated.")
    problem = ancestry_problem(tool, ROOT_UID, ROOT_TOP)
    if problem:
        raise Refused(
            f"{tool} is not a tree this may run as {AGENTS}: {problem}. Nothing further was "
            f"migrated.")
    name, _, sha = agent_platform_tree.name.partition("@")
    if name != "agent-platform" or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{40}", sha):
        raise Refused(
            f"{agent_platform_tree} does not name an agent-platform commit "
            f"(agent-platform@<40 hex>). Nothing further was migrated.")
    bare = agent_platform_tree.parent / "agent-platform.git"
    problem = ancestry_problem(bare, ROOT_UID, ROOT_TOP)
    if problem:
        raise Refused(
            f"{bare} is not a repository this may install from as {AGENTS}: {problem}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    deploy_dir = agents_home / AGENT_PLATFORM_SHARED_REL
    # Mirrors `install_document_service`'s own call: sudo's PATH lacks the
    # install's Python, and git refuses a repository another account owns
    # ("dubious ownership") without `safe.directory` for this one command.
    path = f"{os.path.dirname(root_interpreter(platform))}:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"
    done = run([SUDO, "-u", AGENTS, "-H", "/usr/bin/env", f"PATH={path}", "GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1",
               "GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=safe.directory", f"GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0={bare}",
               str(tool), f"--repo={bare}", f"--ref={sha}", "--allow-non-main",
               f"--deploy-dir={deploy_dir}"],
              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"could not deploy the shared agent-platform area: install-agent-platform-shared "
            f"exited {done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further "
            f"was migrated.")


# --- the document service -------------------------------------------------------


class DirOps(NamedTuple):
    """The directory primitives `ensure_document_socket_dir` calls,
    injectable so a test can record exactly what owner, group and mode this
    script asked for without depending on this process's own privilege to
    really set them, or on whether this kernel keeps the setgid bit for a
    non-root chmod."""
    mkdir: object
    chown: object
    chmod: object
    lstat: object


def _mkdir_with_parents(path: Path, mode: int) -> None:
    Path(path).mkdir(mode=mode, parents=True)


REAL_DIR_OPS = DirOps(mkdir=_mkdir_with_parents, chown=os.chown, chmod=os.chmod, lstat=os.lstat)


def ensure_document_socket_dir(socket_dir: Path, uid: int, gid: int, *,
                               dir_ops: DirOps = REAL_DIR_OPS) -> None:
    """Create `socket_dir`, owned by `uid:gid`, mode 2750 — one of the root
    commands `install-kb-document-api` only prints for an operator to run
    by hand, performed here instead. Never follows a symlink. Idempotent: a
    directory that already has exactly this owner, group and mode is left
    alone. One that differs in any of the three is refused rather than
    corrected — correcting it would be this script deciding, on root's
    behalf, that what is there is wrong.
    """
    try:
        info = dir_ops.lstat(socket_dir)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        dir_ops.mkdir(socket_dir, 0o750)
        dir_ops.chown(socket_dir, uid, gid)
        dir_ops.chmod(socket_dir, KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_MODE)  # mkdir's own mode cannot set the setgid bit
        return
    if stat.S_ISLNK(info.st_mode):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {socket_dir}: it is a symlink. Nothing further was migrated.")
    if not stat.S_ISDIR(info.st_mode):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {socket_dir}: it exists and is not a directory. Nothing further was migrated.")
    actual = (info.st_uid, info.st_gid, stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode))
    wanted = (uid, gid, KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_MODE)
    if actual != wanted:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {socket_dir}: it already exists with owner={info.st_uid} group={info.st_gid} "
            f"mode={oct(stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode))}, not owner={uid} group={gid} "
            f"mode={oct(KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_MODE)}. Resolve by hand. Nothing further was migrated.")


def render_daemon_template_values(agent_platform_tree: Path, agents_home: Path) -> dict:
    """The template keys' values, rendered by root from its own placed
    agent-platform tree's launchd template with `__HOME__` set to `agents`'
    home, exactly as install-kb-document-api renders them."""
    template = agent_platform_tree / KB_DOCUMENT_API_TEMPLATE_REL
    problem = ancestry_problem(template, ROOT_UID, ROOT_TOP)
    if problem:
        raise Refused(f"{template} is not a template this may trust: {problem}. "
                      f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    try:
        text = template.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        rendered = plistlib.loads(text.replace("__HOME__", str(agents_home)).encode("utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise Refused(f"could not read {template}: {exc}. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
    if not isinstance(rendered, dict):
        raise Refused(f"{template} does not hold a dictionary. Nothing further was migrated.")
    return {k: rendered[k] for k in KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_TEMPLATE_KEYS if k in rendered}


def verify_staged_daemon_plist(path: Path, agents_uid: int, orient_group: str,
                               template_values: dict | None = None) -> dict:
    """Refuse a staged LaunchDaemon plist that is not exactly what
    `install-kb-document-api` is trusted to have staged under `agents`' own
    deploy area. Opened exactly once, with `O_NOFOLLOW`, and every check —
    the symlink/regular-file test, the ownership and mode checks, and the
    bytes handed to `plistlib` — reads that one descriptor, never the path
    again, so nothing `agents` does to the path after this call can change
    what was checked.

    Refuses on any key outside the five `install-kb-document-api` itself
    ever writes into this plist (`KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_PLIST_ALLOWED_KEYS`), on
    `Label`, `UserName`, `GroupName` or `Umask` not matching exactly what
    this migration expects, and on `ProgramArguments` not a non-empty list
    of strings all rooted under the deploy directory
    `install-kb-document-api` writes its own code to (`path`'s own parent),
    apart from one `--socket=` argument naming exactly the socket under
    `KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_DIR`.

    Returns the parsed plist on success — `install_document_service` then
    re-serializes this exact dict for the live copy, rather than reading
    `path`'s bytes a second time.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK | os.O_CLOEXEC)
    except OSError as exc:
        if exc.errno == errno.ENOENT:
            raise Refused(
                f"{path} does not exist. install-kb-document-api was supposed to stage it "
                f"there. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
        if exc.errno == errno.ELOOP:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it is a symlink. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
        raise Refused(f"could not open {path}: {exc.strerror}. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
    try:
        info = os.fstat(fd)
        # Checked before any read: `agents` owns this directory and could stage a
        # FIFO instead of a plist, which `O_NONBLOCK` above kept the open from
        # blocking on, but a read would still block waiting for a writer.
        if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
            raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it is not a plain regular file. Nothing further was migrated.")
        if info.st_uid != agents_uid:
            raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: it is not owned by {AGENTS}. Nothing further was migrated.")
        if stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode) & (stat.S_IWGRP | stat.S_IWOTH):
            raise Refused(
                f"refusing {path}: it is group- or other-writable ({oct(stat.S_IMODE(info.st_mode))}). "
                f"Nothing further was migrated.")
        chunks = []
        while True:
            chunk = os.read(fd, 65536)
            if not chunk:
                break
            chunks.append(chunk)
        data = b"".join(chunks)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    try:
        plist = plistlib.loads(data)
    except (ValueError, OSError) as exc:
        raise Refused(f"{path} does not hold a valid plist: {exc}. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
    if not isinstance(plist, dict):
        raise Refused(f"refusing {path}: its top level is not a dictionary. Nothing further was migrated.")
    template_values = template_values or {}
    unknown = sorted(set(plist) - KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_PLIST_ALLOWED_KEYS - set(template_values))
    if unknown:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: it carries key(s) install-kb-document-api never writes into "
            f"this plist: {', '.join(unknown)}. Nothing further was migrated.")
    differing = sorted(k for k in template_values if k in plist and plist[k] != template_values[k])
    if differing:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: {', '.join(differing)} differ(s) from what root renders from its own "
            f"copy of the template. Nothing further was migrated.")
    for key in ("Label", "UserName", "GroupName"):
        value = plist.get(key)
        if not isinstance(value, str):
            raise Refused(
                f"refusing {path}: {key} is {value!r} ({type(value).__name__}), not a string. "
                f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    if plist.get("Label") != KB_DOCUMENT_API_LABEL:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: Label is {plist.get('Label')!r}, not {KB_DOCUMENT_API_LABEL!r}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    if plist.get("UserName") != AGENTS:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: UserName is {plist.get('UserName')!r}, not {AGENTS!r}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    if plist.get("GroupName") != orient_group:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: GroupName is {plist.get('GroupName')!r}, not {orient_group!r}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    umask = plist.get("Umask")
    # `bool` is a subclass of `int`, so `isinstance(True, int)` passes; checked apart to
    # keep `Umask: true` from reading as the integer 1. A plist `<real>` deserializes to
    # `float`, which this excludes too, since `23.0 == 23` would otherwise pass the
    # equality check below while launchd keeps requiring an integer.
    if not isinstance(umask, int) or isinstance(umask, bool):
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: Umask is {umask!r} ({type(umask).__name__}), not an integer. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    if umask != KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_UMASK:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: Umask is {umask!r}, not {KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_UMASK} "
            f"(octal 027). Nothing further was migrated.")
    program_arguments = plist.get("ProgramArguments")
    if (not isinstance(program_arguments, list) or not program_arguments
            or not all(isinstance(arg, str) for arg in program_arguments)):
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: ProgramArguments is {program_arguments!r}, not a non-empty "
            f"list of strings. Nothing further was migrated.")
    deploy_dir = Path(os.path.normpath(str(path.parent)))
    # agent-platform's launchd template names the socket explicitly, so every
    # staged plist carries this one argument outside the deploy directory. It
    # is accepted only as root's own socket path, the directory
    # ensure_document_socket_dir() creates.
    socket_arg = f"--socket={KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_DIR / KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_NAME}"
    outside = []
    for arg in program_arguments:
        if arg == socket_arg:
            continue
        if not os.path.isabs(arg):
            outside.append(arg)
            continue
        normalized = Path(os.path.normpath(arg))
        if normalized != deploy_dir and deploy_dir not in normalized.parents:
            outside.append(arg)
    if outside:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing {path}: ProgramArguments names {outside!r} outside {deploy_dir}, where "
            f"install-kb-document-api deploys its own code. Nothing further was migrated.")
    return plist


def confirm_document_service_deployed(tool: Path, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """Confirm, with `install-kb-document-api --status` run as `agents`,
    that a commit is actually deployed."""
    done = run([SUDO, "-u", AGENTS, "-H", str(tool), "--status"],
              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"install-kb-document-api --status exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()} — no commit confirmed deployed. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    deployed = any(line.startswith("commit:") and line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
                  for line in (done.stdout or "").splitlines())
    if not deployed:
        raise Refused(
            f"install-kb-document-api --status printed no commit: {done.stdout!r}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")


def install_document_service(agent_platform_tree: Path, orient_group: str, orient_gid: int,
                             agents_home: Path, agents_uid: int, platform: str,
                             socket_dir: Path = KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_DIR,
                             system_dir: str | None = None,
                             dir_ops: DirOps = REAL_DIR_OPS,
                             run=subprocess.run, sleep=time.sleep) -> None:
    """Install the document service with Orient's own group as
    `--client-group`, then do, as root, what `bin/install-kb-document-api`
    only prints for an operator rather than doing itself, because that
    installer loads nothing and runs no sudo: create the socket directory,
    copy the staged LaunchDaemon plist into the supervisor's system
    directory, and bootstrap it, then confirm a commit is actually deployed.

    Refuses outright on Linux: the document service has no Linux shape
    designed yet, so there is nothing here to perform as root.

    The installer itself is run as `agents`, never as root directly: the
    service runs under the account that runs `agentctl`, which is `agents`
    on every shape this script handles — the same way `place-orient` drops
    to Orient's own account before calling `install-backend` rather than
    letting that command run as root. `--repo` and `--ref` are passed
    explicitly, naming root's own bare copy at the commit of the placed
    agent-platform tree the rest of this migration already uses, rather
    than whatever checkout the installer would otherwise fall back to.
    """
    if platform != "macos":
        raise Refused(
            "install_document_service: refusing on Linux — the document service has no "
            "Linux shape designed yet (/var/db and launchd are macOS-only here). Nothing "
            "further was migrated.")
    tool = agent_platform_tree / "bin" / "install-kb-document-api"
    if not tool.is_file():
        raise Refused(f"{tool} does not exist in the placed agent-platform tree. Nothing further was migrated.")
    problem = ancestry_problem(tool, ROOT_UID, ROOT_TOP)
    if problem:
        raise Refused(
            f"{tool} is not a tree this may run as {AGENTS}: {problem}. Nothing further was "
            f"migrated.")
    # The installer reads a git ref with `git archive`, never a working tree,
    # so it is pointed at root's own bare copy at the commit this install
    # records for agent-platform (the placed tree's own `@<sha>`), not at the
    # extracted tree, which is not a repository. The r45 macOS rehearsal
    # showed the three things this line answers: install-kb-document-api
    # parses `--name=value` only, and refused `--client-group <group>` as an
    # unknown option; `main` resolves in neither root's bare copy nor agents'
    # release clone, so the release's own commit is named, with
    # `--allow-non-main`, the installer's override for that; and git refuses
    # a repository another account owns ("dubious ownership"), so
    # `safe.directory` names this one bare copy, for this one command, as
    # command-line configuration.
    name, _, sha = agent_platform_tree.name.partition("@")
    if name != "agent-platform" or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{40}", sha):
        raise Refused(
            f"{agent_platform_tree} does not name an agent-platform commit "
            f"(agent-platform@<40 hex>). Nothing further was migrated.")
    bare = agent_platform_tree.parent / "agent-platform.git"
    problem = ancestry_problem(bare, ROOT_UID, ROOT_TOP)
    if problem:
        raise Refused(
            f"{bare} is not a repository this may install from as {AGENTS}: {problem}. "
            f"Nothing further was migrated.")
    # sudo's own PATH lacks the install's Python (Homebrew's python3.11 on
    # macOS), which the installer finds on PATH; r45 stopped on
    # "python3.11 not found on PATH" until its directory came first.
    path = f"{os.path.dirname(root_interpreter(platform))}:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"
    done = run([SUDO, "-u", AGENTS, "-H", "/usr/bin/env", f"PATH={path}", "GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1",
               "GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=safe.directory", f"GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0={bare}",
               str(tool), f"--client-group={orient_group}", f"--repo={bare}",
               f"--ref={sha}", "--allow-non-main"],
              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"install-kb-document-api --client-group {orient_group} exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. Nothing further was migrated.")

    ensure_document_socket_dir(socket_dir, agents_uid, orient_gid, dir_ops=dir_ops)

    staged_plist = agents_home / KB_DOCUMENT_DAEMON_PLIST_REL
    validated_plist = verify_staged_daemon_plist(
        staged_plist, agents_uid, orient_group,
        template_values=render_daemon_template_values(agent_platform_tree, agents_home))

    live_dir = Path(system_dir or LAUNCHD_SYSTEM_DIR)
    live_plist = live_dir / f"{KB_DOCUMENT_API_LABEL}.plist"
    # Serialized from the dict `verify_staged_daemon_plist` already parsed
    # and checked, never read from `staged_plist` a second time: `agents`
    # owns that path throughout, so a second read could see bytes swapped in
    # after the check above passed.
    data = plistlib.dumps(validated_plist)
    flags = os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC | os.O_NOFOLLOW
    try:
        handle = os.open(live_plist, flags, 0o644)
    except OSError as exc:
        if exc.errno == errno.ELOOP:
            raise Refused(
                f"refusing to write {live_plist}: it is a symlink. Nothing further was "
                f"migrated.") from exc
        raise Refused(f"could not write {live_plist}: {exc.strerror}. Nothing further was migrated.") from exc
    with os.fdopen(handle, "wb") as out:
        out.write(data)

    # **THE BOOTSTRAP IS RETRIED, BOUNDED, BECAUSE A BOOTOUT CAN RETURN BEFORE
    # LAUNCHD WILL TAKE THE SAME LABEL BACK.** Re-running stand-up-backend on a
    # running three-account macOS install (rehearsal r57, 2026-11-25, twice in
    # a row) booted the daemon out and then had this bootstrap exit 5
    # ("Input/output error"), leaving the document service unloaded. A plain
    # bootstrap of the same plist straight after the refusal succeeded on its
    # first try, so the refusal is transient; a first install, with nothing
    # to boot out, never hits it.
    run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"system/{KB_DOCUMENT_API_LABEL}"], capture_output=True, check=False)
    for attempt in range(1, BOOTSTRAP_ATTEMPTS + 1):
        done = run(["launchctl", "bootstrap", "system", str(live_plist)],
                  capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        if done.returncode == 0:
            break
        if attempt < BOOTSTRAP_ATTEMPTS:
            sleep(BOOTSTRAP_RETRY_SECONDS)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"launchctl bootstrap system {live_plist} failed on all {BOOTSTRAP_ATTEMPTS} tries; "
            f"the last exited {done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}. The document "
            f"service is not loaded now; `sudo launchctl bootstrap system {live_plist}` loads "
            f"it. Nothing further was migrated.")

    confirm_document_service_deployed(tool, run=run)


def orient_job_declares_document_socket(jobs_dir: Path = REPO / "jobs") -> bool:
    """Whether `jobs/orient.toml`'s own `[account-environment]` carries
    `ORIENT_KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET` — the variable convivy-orient's own
    `kb_commit.py` reads fresh on each call (`_SERVICE_SOCKET_ENV`) to reach
    the document service `install_document_service` just installed. Read
    directly from the declaration rather than assumed, so `migrate()`'s own
    report of a document-service install never outruns what Orient's job
    actually tells its restarted process to connect to.
    """
    job = declaration.load(jobs_dir / "orient.toml")
    return bool(job.account_environment.get("ORIENT_KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET"))


# --- cloudflared ------------------------------------------------------------------


_CLOUDFLARED_SERVICE_RE = re.compile(r"^(\s*service:\s*)(\S+)(\s*)$")


def repoint_cloudflared(config_path: Path, socket_path: str = ORIENT_SOCKET_PATH) -> bool:
    """Replace the one ingress rule's `service:` line that still points at
    Orient's old loopback address with `unix:<socket_path>`, line for line,
    leaving every other line byte for byte. Returns whether it changed
    anything. Refuses, naming the file, when no such line is there to
    replace and the socket line is not already in place either — this never
    guesses which of several `service:` lines is Orient's own when more
    than one could match, since `cloudflared`'s config has no stdlib parser
    this codebase already depends on elsewhere.
    """
    if not config_path.is_file():
        raise Refused(f"{config_path} does not exist. Nothing further was migrated.")
    wanted = f"unix:{socket_path}"
    lines = config_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines(keepends=True)
    changed = False
    already = False
    new_lines = []
    for line in lines:
        match = _CLOUDFLARED_SERVICE_RE.match(line.rstrip("\n"))
        if match and match.group(2) in ("http://127.0.0.1:8099", wanted):
            if match.group(2) == wanted:
                already = True
                new_lines.append(line)
                continue
            newline = "\n" if line.endswith("\n") else ""
            new_lines.append(f"{match.group(1)}{wanted}{match.group(3)}{newline}")
            changed = True
        else:
            new_lines.append(line)
    if not changed and not already:
        raise Refused(
            f"{config_path} names no ingress rule pointed at Orient's old loopback address "
            f"(http://127.0.0.1:8099), and none already at {wanted}. Refusing to guess which "
            f"line is Orient's own. Nothing further was migrated.")
    if changed:
        _write_cloudflared_config(config_path, "".join(new_lines))
    return changed


def validate_cloudflared(config_path: Path, operator: str, run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    done = run([SUDO, "-u", operator, "-H", "cloudflared", "tunnel", "--config", str(config_path),
               "ingress", "validate"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"cloudflared tunnel ingress validate exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()} against {config_path}. Nothing further "
            f"was migrated.")


def restart_cloudflared(operator: str, operator_uid: int, platform: str, label: str,
                        run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    if platform == "macos":
        done = run(["launchctl", "kickstart", "-k", f"gui/{operator_uid}/{label}"],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    else:
        done = run(user_manager_argv(operator, operator_uid, "restart", label),
                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        note = (f" {operator}'s own user manager has to be running for this — "
                f"`loginctl enable-linger {operator}` starts it at boot."
                if platform == "linux" else "")
        raise Refused(
            f"could not restart cloudflared ({label}): exited {done.returncode}: "
            f"{(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}.{note} Nothing further was migrated.")


def _with_cloudflared_note(message: str, note: str) -> str:
    """`message`'s own "Nothing further was migrated"/"Nothing was
    migrated" ending is false once rollback has gone on to restore
    cloudflared's config, so replace it with `note` — capitalized, as the
    start of a new sentence — rather than leaving both claims standing
    side by side."""
    corrected = f"{note[0].upper()}{note[1:]}."
    for suffix in ("Nothing further was migrated.", "Nothing was migrated."):
        if message.endswith(suffix):
            return f"{message[:-len(suffix)].rstrip()} {corrected}"
    return f"{message} {corrected}"


# --- the migration itself -------------------------------------------------------


def migrate(agents_home: Path, shape: str, platform: str, *, operator: str,
            cloudflared_label: str = DEFAULT_CLOUDFLARED_LABEL,
            privileged_prefix: Path = PRIVILEGED_PREFIX,
            document_socket_dir: Path = KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET_DIR,
            document_dir_ops: DirOps = REAL_DIR_OPS,
            jobs_dir: Path = REPO / "jobs",
            port_connects=_real_port_connects,
            person_save_sources: tuple[str, str, str, list[str] | None] | None = None,
            run=subprocess.run, report=print, system_dir: str | None = None) -> None:
    """Move Orient's state and credentials from `agents` to its own
    account, as the module docstring's own "WHAT IT DOES, IN ORDER" lays
    out. Raises `Refused` on any failure before the two tokens are rotated,
    after rolling back whatever this run had already written, restoring
    any unit this run removed, and restarting any unit this run stopped. A
    failure from the rotation onward is reported rather than unwound — see
    the module docstring's own "ROLLBACK".

    `privileged_prefix` and `document_socket_dir` default to this
    machine's real root-owned locations; a test overrides both, the same
    way `system_dir` is already overridden, so nothing here ever touches
    a real `/opt/builder` or `/var/db` outside a real install.
    `document_dir_ops` defaults to the real `os.mkdir`/`chown`/`chmod`/
    `lstat`; a test overrides it so the socket directory's owner, group and
    mode are only ever recorded, never really set. `jobs_dir` defaults to
    this repository's own `jobs/`, where `orient_job_declares_document_socket`
    reads `orient.toml` from; a test overrides it to exercise the report
    this produces when that declaration does not carry the variable.

    `person_save_sources`, when given, is `(root_ca, app_key, app_config,
    persons)` — the operator's own sources for the Library's person-save
    inputs, the same four `main()` reads from `--root-ca`, `--app-key`,
    `--app-config` and `--persons`. `None` skips placing them and says so;
    it never refuses the rest of the migration, since saving was already
    unavailable before this ran.
    """
    orient_name = orient_account_name(platform)
    actual_system_dir = system_dir or (LAUNCHD_SYSTEM_DIR if platform == "macos" else SYSTEMD_SYSTEM_DIR)
    agents_ids = account_ids(AGENTS)
    assert agents_ids is not None
    stopped = False
    root: str | None = None
    removed_units: dict[Path, bytes] = {}
    removed_watch: dict[Path, bytes] = {}
    cloudflared_file_written = False
    cloudflared_restarted = False
    cloudflared_config_path: Path | None = None
    cloudflared_original_text: str | None = None
    operator_ids: tuple[int, int] | None = None

    def rollback(reason: Exception) -> str | None:
        """Undo whatever this run already did, and return a sentence
        describing what it restored on cloudflared's own behalf, or
        `None` when there was nothing to restore there. The caller folds
        that sentence into the message it raises, since `reason` on its
        own would otherwise still claim nothing further was migrated even
        once this has written the operator's cloudflared config back."""
        if removed_watch:
            restore_deploy_watch(platform, agents_ids[0], removed_watch, run=run)
        if removed_units:
            restore_agents_units(removed_units)
        restarted_ok = not stopped
        if stopped:
            labels = existing_orient_labels(platform, actual_system_dir)
            restart_problem = start_orient_units(str(agents_home), root or "", platform, labels, run=run)
            if restart_problem:
                if cloudflared_file_written:
                    report(f"{PROG}: rolling back, but Orient's own units could not be restarted, "
                           f"so cloudflared is left pointed at the socket; the site stays dark "
                           f"until Orient's old units are restarted by hand and cloudflared is "
                           f"repointed at unix:{ORIENT_SOCKET_PATH} by hand.")
                raise Refused(
                    f"{reason} Rolling back, but Orient's units could not be restarted "
                    f"either: {restart_problem}. Start them by hand.") from reason
            restarted_ok = True
        if not (cloudflared_file_written and restarted_ok):
            return None
        assert cloudflared_config_path is not None and cloudflared_original_text is not None
        assert operator_ids is not None
        if not stopped:
            # No Orient unit has stopped on this path — the failure that
            # triggered this rollback was validate_cloudflared's or
            # restart_cloudflared's own, inside the same step that wrote
            # the file, before step 5 stops anything. The old,
            # agents'-own Orient is still serving the port throughout, so
            # there is nothing to confirm: put the file back, and restart
            # cloudflared again only if it was actually restarted onto
            # the new setting — if that restart never happened, the live
            # process never picked up the write, and the file is all
            # that needs reverting.
            if cloudflared_restarted:
                revert_cloudflared(cloudflared_config_path, cloudflared_original_text, operator,
                                   operator_ids[0], platform, cloudflared_label, run=run)
                note = (f"restored {cloudflared_config_path} to its own original setting "
                        f"and restarted cloudflared")
            else:
                _write_cloudflared_config(cloudflared_config_path, cloudflared_original_text)
                note = f"restored {cloudflared_config_path} to its own original setting"
            report(f"{PROG}: {note}")
            return note
        confirmed, holder = confirm_restored_orient_holds_port(platform, run=run,
                                                               port_connects=port_connects)
        if confirmed:
            revert_cloudflared(cloudflared_config_path, cloudflared_original_text, operator,
                               operator_ids[0], platform, cloudflared_label, run=run)
            note = f"rolled cloudflared back to {cloudflared_config_path}'s own original setting"
            report(f"{PROG}: {note}")
            return note
        named = f" {holder}." if holder else " Nothing holds it yet."
        report(f"{PROG}: rolling back, but the restored Orient does not yet hold "
               f"127.0.0.1:{ORIENT_OLD_LOOPBACK_PORT}, so cloudflared is left pointed at "
               f"the socket; the site stays dark.{named}")
        return None

    try:
        root = agents_builder_root(str(agents_home), run=run)
        # Checked first, before anything moves at all: starting the root
        # deploy step this early, before Orient's own system unit exists,
        # is what a run against an install whose Orient main is ahead of
        # the deployed commit refused on (bin/orient-deploy placed the new
        # commit, then its restart of com.convivy.orient.service failed,
        # Unit not found, and the swap-back failed the same way) — see
        # start_root_deploy_step below, called only once Orient's own
        # units are confirmed running again, near the end of this
        # function.
        if not root_deploy_step_loaded(platform, run=run):
            raise Refused(
                f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} is not loaded in the supervisor. That is "
                f"bin/orient-deploy's own job, declared in "
                f"jobs/follow-orient-main/orient-deploy.toml. Install and load it first. "
                f"Nothing was migrated.")

        following_main = follows_orient_main(privileged_prefix)
        deployed_sha: str | None = None
        if following_main:
            deployed_sha = read_deployed_commit(privileged_prefix)
            if deployed_sha is None:
                raise Refused(
                    f"this install follows Orient's main, and "
                    f"{privileged_prefix / ORIENT_DEPLOY_RECORD_REL} names no deployed commit, "
                    f"so there is nothing to place under {orient_name}'s own account before it "
                    f"restarts. Nothing was migrated.")

        created_account = ensure_orient_account(shape, platform, run=run)
        if created_account:
            report(f"{PROG}: created the {orient_name} account")

        orient_home_str = account_home(orient_name)
        if orient_home_str is None:
            raise Refused(f"{orient_name} still does not exist after provisioning. Nothing was migrated.")
        orient_home = Path(orient_home_str)
        orient_ids = account_ids(orient_name)
        assert orient_ids is not None
        if not owned_by(orient_home_str, orient_ids[0]):
            raise Refused(
                f"{orient_home} (home of {orient_name}) is not owned by its own account. "
                f"Nothing was migrated.")

        # Resolved here, not at the end: the repoint below needs the
        # operator's own cloudflared config before any job stops.
        operator_home_str = account_home(operator)
        operator_ids = account_ids(operator)
        if operator_home_str is None or operator_ids is None:
            raise Refused(
                f"could not resolve the operator account ({operator!r}), so there is nowhere "
                f"to repoint cloudflared before any job stops. Nothing was migrated.")

        # Repointed and restarted before any job stops, so the tunnel
        # never forwards to Orient's old loopback address while Orient is
        # down: a request fails rather than reaches another account until
        # Orient listens on its own socket.
        cloudflared_config_path = Path(operator_home_str) / CLOUDFLARED_CONFIG_REL
        try:
            cloudflared_original_text = cloudflared_config_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except OSError as exc:
            raise Refused(
                f"could not read {cloudflared_config_path}: {exc}. Nothing was migrated.") from exc
        changed = repoint_cloudflared(cloudflared_config_path)
        if changed:
            cloudflared_file_written = True
            validate_cloudflared(cloudflared_config_path, operator, run=run)
            restart_cloudflared(operator, operator_ids[0], platform, cloudflared_label, run=run)
            cloudflared_restarted = True
            report(f"{PROG}: repointed {cloudflared_config_path} at unix:{ORIENT_SOCKET_PATH} and "
                   f"restarted cloudflared, before stopping any of Orient's own jobs")
        else:
            report(f"{PROG}: {cloudflared_config_path} already names Orient's own socket")

        stopped = True
        stop_orient_units(str(agents_home), root, platform, run=run)

        remove_agents_units(agents_home, platform, actual_system_dir, removed_units, run=run)
        remove_deploy_watch(platform, agents_home, agents_ids[0], removed_watch, run=run)

        place_orient_code(platform, run=run)

        library_name = library_account_name(platform)
        library_home_str = account_home(library_name)
        if library_home_str is None:
            raise Refused(
                f"{library_name} does not exist. Run bin/migrate-library-account first. "
                f"Nothing was migrated.")
        library_home = Path(library_home_str)
        library_ids = account_ids(library_name)
        assert library_ids is not None
        # Both mints below run after the rotation, past the point of no
        # return, so the capabilities they withhold are computed here,
        # while a failure still rolls back. A rehearsal of this migration
        # failed computing them after the rotation, leaving the install
        # with rotated tokens and Orient stopped.
        capture_withholds = run_as_account(
            *library_ids, lambda: library_role_withholds(library_home, CAPTURE_TOKEN_GRANT, run=run))
        person_save_withholds = run_as_account(
            *library_ids, lambda: library_role_withholds(library_home, KB_PERSON_SAVE_GRANT, run=run))
    except Exception as exc:
        cloudflared_note = rollback(exc)
        # `exc`'s own message, in the Refused case, still ends "Nothing
        # further was migrated"/"Nothing was migrated" — true when this
        # is caught before cloudflared's config was ever written, false
        # once rollback has just put it back; `_with_cloudflared_note`
        # corrects that ending only when there is something to correct.
        if isinstance(exc, Refused):
            if cloudflared_note:
                raise Refused(_with_cloudflared_note(str(exc), cloudflared_note)) from exc
            raise
        message = f"refusing — {exc}. Nothing was migrated."
        if cloudflared_note:
            message = _with_cloudflared_note(message, cloudflared_note)
        raise Refused(message) from exc

    # Past this point Orient's own account holds real data — the account is
    # provisioned, its code is placed, and the tokens are about to be
    # rotated — so failures from here on are reported, not unwound — the
    # state they would be undoing may already be verified and promoted by
    # the time they happen. This mirrors bin/migrate-library-account's own
    # split between its reversible setup and its one-way promotion.
    for store_file, out_name in zip(ORIENT_TOKEN_STORE_FILES, ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES):
        out_path = library_home / ORIENT_CONTROL / out_name
        rotate_orient_token(library_home, library_ids, store_file, out_path, run=run)
        report(f"{PROG}: rotated {out_name} in the {store_file} store")

    if mint_capture_token(library_home, library_ids, run=run, withholds=capture_withholds):
        report(f"{PROG}: minted Orient's own narrow signal credential ({CAPTURE_TOKEN_NAME})")

    # Checked before anything below decides whether to mint: an install or
    # an earlier run of this migration may already hold `kb-person-save`
    # under a crew, which authenticates nobody where the install's project
    # registry has no row for it, whether or not `agents` has a copy — see
    # `remint_kb_person_save_crew`'s own docstring.
    reminted_person_save = remint_kb_person_save_crew(library_home, library_ids, run=run)
    if reminted_person_save:
        report(f"{PROG}: re-minted {KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME} with no crew; a crew the "
               f"project registry has no row for makes the Library refuse it")

    # Checked against `agents`' own copy, never the Library's: the Library
    # may already hold one from a fresh install's `jobs/library.toml` own
    # `prepare`, minted under this same release before this migration ever
    # ran, while `agents` has not yet received it — see
    # `mint_kb_person_save_token`'s own docstring.
    agents_oc = agents_orient_control(agents_home)
    deliver_person_save = reminted_person_save or not (agents_oc / KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME).exists()
    if not reminted_person_save and deliver_person_save and mint_kb_person_save_token(
            library_home, library_ids, run=run, withholds=person_save_withholds):
        report(f"{PROG}: minted {KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME}")
    elif not deliver_person_save:
        report(f"{PROG}: {AGENTS} already holds {KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME}; left it alone")

    deliver_to_orient(run=run)
    report(f"{PROG}: delivered the rotated tokens and {CAPTURE_TOKEN_NAME} to {orient_home / ORIENT_CONTROL}")
    if deliver_person_save:
        report(f"{PROG}: delivered {KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME} to {agents_oc / KB_PERSON_SAVE_TOKEN_NAME}")

    for name in ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES:
        with contextlib.suppress(FileNotFoundError):
            os.remove(agents_oc / name)
    report(f"{PROG}: removed {AGENTS}'s own, now-superseded copies of {', '.join(ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES)}")

    # agents' own old, wide capture-token (minted under the name
    # github-capture) is gone now that Orient's own narrow one is
    # delivered: absence changes nothing, and the operator still revokes
    # its credential by hand, since deleting this file is not revoking it.
    agents_capture = agents_oc / CAPTURE_TOKEN_NAME
    with contextlib.suppress(FileNotFoundError):
        os.remove(agents_capture)
        report(f"{PROG}: removed {agents_capture}; revoke its credential once this migration "
               f"has finished")

    try:
        copied: list[str] = []
        copy_flat_files(agents_oc, orient_home / ORIENT_CONTROL, ORIENT_CONTROL_OTHER_NAMES,
                        *orient_ids, agents_ids[0], copied)
        for name in copied:
            os.remove(agents_oc / name)
        if copied:
            report(f"{PROG}: moved {', '.join(copied)} to {orient_home / ORIENT_CONTROL}")

        src_config = agents_home / ORIENT_CONFIG_REL
        if src_config.is_dir():
            dst_config_parent = orient_home / ORIENT_CONFIG_REL.parent
            dst_config = orient_home / ORIENT_CONFIG_REL
            if os.path.lexists(dst_config):
                raise Refused(
                    f"{dst_config} already exists, and {src_config} is still there too. This "
                    f"looks like a previous migration that did not finish cleanly. Resolve by "
                    f"hand.")
            mkdir_owned(dst_config_parent, *orient_ids)
            staging = dst_config_parent / f".{ORIENT_CONFIG_REL.name}.migrating-{os.getpid()}"
            manifest: dict[str, tuple[int, str]] = {}
            src_fd = os.open(str(src_config), DIR_OPEN)
            try:
                if os.fstat(src_fd).st_uid != agents_ids[0]:
                    raise Refused(f"refusing {src_config}: it is not owned by {AGENTS}.")
                os.mkdir(staging, 0o700)
                dst_fd = os.open(str(staging), DIR_OPEN)
                try:
                    os.fchown(dst_fd, *orient_ids)
                    copy_tree(src_fd, dst_fd, str(src_config), *orient_ids, manifest)
                finally:
                    os.close(dst_fd)
            finally:
                os.close(src_fd)
            verify_tree_copy(manifest, staging)
            os.rename(staging, dst_config)
            shutil.rmtree(src_config)
            report(f"{PROG}: moved {src_config} to {dst_config}")
    except Refused as exc:
        remaining = remaining_agents_orient_files(agents_home)
        raise Refused(
            f"{exc} Still under {AGENTS}'s own home: "
            f"{', '.join(remaining) if remaining else 'nothing from the table above'}. Delete "
            f"each by hand once {orient_name}'s own copy is confirmed.") from exc

    try:
        moved_logs = move_sibling_logs(agents_home, orient_home, agents_ids[0], orient_ids)
        if moved_logs:
            report(f"{PROG}: moved {', '.join(moved_logs)}")
    except OSError as exc:
        report(f"{PROG}: migrated, but could not move every sibling log: {exc}. Move "
               f"{agents_home / STATE_REL} by hand.")

    deploy_dir = agents_home / DEPLOY_ORIENT_REL
    if os.path.lexists(deploy_dir) and not os.path.islink(deploy_dir):
        shutil.rmtree(deploy_dir, ignore_errors=True)
        report(f"{PROG}: removed {deploy_dir}")

    if following_main:
        # place_orient_code() (bin/place-orient) left Orient's own code
        # untouched on this install, deferring to the root deploy step —
        # which is not started until Orient's own units are confirmed
        # restarted, near the end of this function. Nothing else would
        # put Orient's own code under its own account before that first
        # cycle, so this places it now, before the statement key below
        # needs Orient's own deploy-area Python to exist.
        assert deployed_sha is not None
        place_deployed_commit(privileged_prefix, deployed_sha, orient_name, platform, run=run)
        report(f"{PROG}: placed {deployed_sha} — the root deploy step's own deployed commit — "
               f"under {orient_home / DEPLOY_ORIENT_REL}, since nothing else would before its "
               f"first cycle after this migration")

    installed_record = privileged_prefix / "src" / "installed"
    record_text = None
    try:
        record_text = installed_record.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass
    sha = None
    if record_text:
        for line in record_text.splitlines():
            fields = line.split()
            if len(fields) == 2 and fields[0] == "convivy-orient":
                sha = fields[1]
    orient_key_line = ""
    if sha is None:
        report(f"{PROG}: migrated the account and its data, but could not find Orient's own "
               f"placed tree to run orient-make-statement-key from. Run it by hand once "
               f"{installed_record} names a convivy-orient commit.")
    else:
        # The release's own convivy-orient tree, which the installer always
        # extracts, never the followed repository's: on a follow-main install
        # src/installed can name a followed commit root never extracted.
        tree = privileged_prefix / "src" / f"convivy-orient@{sha}"
        # Orient's own deploy area, not root's system Python: the tool
        # needs cryptography, which root's system interpreter does not
        # carry and which only Orient's own venv does, placed either by
        # place_orient_code() above or, on a follow-main install, by
        # place_deployed_commit() just above.
        python = orient_home / DEPLOY_ORIENT_REL / ".venv" / "bin" / "python"
        orient_key_line = run_orient_make_statement_key(orient_home, orient_ids, tree, python, run=run)
        if orient_key_line:
            report(f"{PROG}: created Orient's own statement key")
        place_statement_key_in_verifier(Path(operator_home_str), operator_ids, orient_key_line)
        report(f"{PROG}: placed the statement key's public half under orient_statement_key "
               f"in {Path(operator_home_str) / KB_VERIFIER_REL}")

    if person_save_sources is None:
        report(f"{PROG}: migrated, but the Library's person-save inputs were not placed: pass "
               f"--root-ca, --app-key, --app-config and --persons to place them. Until they are "
               f"placed, the Library refuses every person-save, Orient-signed or Deck-signed.")
    else:
        root_ca, app_key, app_config, persons = person_save_sources
        try:
            placed = place_library_person_save_inputs(
                library_home, library_ids, root_ca, app_key, app_config, persons,
                key=orient_key_line or None)
        except Refused as exc:
            report(f"{PROG}: migrated, but the Library's person-save inputs were not placed: {exc}")
        else:
            for name, what in placed:
                report(f"{PROG}: person-save {name}: {what}")

    agent_platform_tree = None
    if record_text:
        for line in record_text.splitlines():
            fields = line.split()
            if len(fields) == 2 and fields[0] == "agent-platform":
                agent_platform_tree = privileged_prefix / "src" / f"agent-platform@{fields[1]}"
    orient_group = "staff" if platform != "macos" else "_orient"
    try:
        orient_gid = orient_ids[1]
        import grp
        orient_group = grp.getgrgid(orient_gid).gr_name
    except (KeyError, ImportError, OSError):
        pass
    if agent_platform_tree is None:
        report(f"{PROG}: migrated, but could not find agent-platform's own placed tree to "
               f"install the document service from. Install it by hand once "
               f"{installed_record} names an agent-platform commit.")
    else:
        try:
            install_agent_platform_shared(agent_platform_tree, agents_home, platform, run=run)
        except Refused as exc:
            report(f"{PROG}: migrated the account and its data, but the shared agent-platform "
                   f"area was not deployed, so the document service was not installed either: "
                   f"{exc}")
        else:
            report(f"{PROG}: deployed the shared agent-platform area under "
                   f"{agents_home / AGENT_PLATFORM_SHARED_REL}")
            try:
                install_document_service(agent_platform_tree, orient_group, orient_gid,
                                          agents_home, agents_ids[0], platform,
                                          socket_dir=document_socket_dir, system_dir=actual_system_dir,
                                          dir_ops=document_dir_ops, run=run)
            except Refused as exc:
                report(f"{PROG}: migrated the account and its data, but the document service was "
                       f"not installed: {exc}")
            else:
                if orient_job_declares_document_socket(jobs_dir):
                    report(f"{PROG}: installed the document service with --client-group {orient_group}, "
                           f"its socket directory, and its LaunchDaemon")
                else:
                    report(f"{PROG}: installed the document service, but jobs/orient.toml names no "
                           f"ORIENT_KB_DOCUMENT_SOCKET in its own [account-environment], so Orient's "
                           f"restart will leave document saves dark. Add the variable there and "
                           f"restart Orient by hand.")

    # Render Orient's own units from the root tree's declarations before
    # starting them: the agents' units were removed above, and
    # install-jobs --start only activates a unit already on disk, so
    # nothing would be there to start without this. Every declared label,
    # not existing_orient_labels() — see start_orient_units and
    # render_orient_units.
    restart_problem = render_orient_units(str(agents_home), root, platform, privileged_prefix, run=run)
    if restart_problem is not None:
        restart_problem = f"could not render its units: {restart_problem}"
    else:
        restart_problem = start_orient_units(str(agents_home), root, platform, ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS, run=run)
    if restart_problem:
        report(f"{PROG}: migrated, but could not restart Orient: {restart_problem}. "
               f"Start it by hand.")
    else:
        report(f"{PROG}: restarted Orient")
        # Only now, with Orient's own units confirmed running again, does
        # this start the root deploy step — see the early check this
        # function already made, before anything was rotated, and the
        # module docstring's own note on why starting it this early fails
        # when Orient main is ahead of the deployed commit. This is already
        # past the point of no return, so a failure here is reported, not
        # rolled back, and never leaves the timer or the service running.
        try:
            start_root_deploy_step(platform, run=run)
        except Refused as exc:
            stop_root_deploy_step(platform, run=run)
            start_by_hand = (f"sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}"
                             if platform == "macos"
                             else f"sudo systemctl start {ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}.timer")
            report(f"{PROG}: migrated and restarted Orient, but {ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL} could "
                   f"not be started: {exc} Once Orient is confirmed serving, start it by hand: "
                   f"{start_by_hand}")
        else:
            report(f"{PROG}: started {ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}")


def person_save_flags_problem(root_ca: str | None, app_key: str | None, app_config: str | None,
                               persons: str | None) -> str | None:
    """None when `--root-ca`, `--app-key`, `--app-config` and `--persons` are
    all given or none is; otherwise names which of the four are still
    missing, for `main()` to refuse with before anything changes or any job
    stops. `orient-statement-key.json` needs all four (step 14), so a
    partial set is refused here rather than treated as none, the way
    `person_save_sources` below already treats a missing fourth."""
    values = {"--root-ca": root_ca, "--app-key": app_key, "--app-config": app_config,
              "--persons": persons}
    given = [name for name, value in values.items() if value]
    if not given or len(given) == len(values):
        return None
    missing = [name for name in values if name not in given]
    return ", ".join(missing)


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description="Move an existing install's Orient to its own service account, rotating "
                    "its two Library tokens.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--shape", required=True, choices=("two", "three"))
    parser.add_argument("--operator", help="the operator's login (default: the user who ran sudo)")
    parser.add_argument("--cloudflared-label", default=DEFAULT_CLOUDFLARED_LABEL,
                        help=f"the operator's cloudflared job label (default: {DEFAULT_CLOUDFLARED_LABEL})")
    parser.add_argument("--system-dir", help="where a system-scope job's unit lives, "
                        "instead of /Library/LaunchDaemons or /etc/systemd/system")
    parser.add_argument("--deploy-signals-bound", action="store_true",
                        help="assert that Orient's crew has already bound the instruction "
                             "block reading deploy_failure and deploy_stall to its own "
                             "sessions (spec step 7). Required for a real run; this script "
                             "cannot verify it itself.")
    parser.add_argument("--root-ca", help="Deck's root certificate (PEM), for the Library's "
                        "person-save inputs")
    parser.add_argument("--app-key", help="the Library App's RSA private key (PEM), for the "
                        "Library's person-save inputs")
    parser.add_argument("--app-config", help="JSON naming the Library App's app_id and its "
                        "repo, for the Library's person-save inputs")
    parser.add_argument("--persons", help="comma-separated emails allowed to sign a person-save, "
                        "for the Library's person-save inputs")
    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="say what would happen, change nothing")
    mode.add_argument("--check", action="store_true", help="report only; 1 if migration is pending")
    mode.add_argument("--status", action="store_true", help="the same report, changing nothing")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    problem = person_save_flags_problem(args.root_ca, args.app_key, args.app_config, args.persons)
    if problem:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — missing {problem}; give --root-ca, --app-key, --app-config "
              f"and --persons together, or omit them all to skip placing the Library's "
              f"person-save inputs. Nothing was migrated.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — this needs root, to stop Orient's units and move both "
              f"accounts' files. Nothing was migrated.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    # Root starts in `/`, as stand-up-backend does. Every `sudo -u <account>`
    # child below inherits this directory, and the operator's own is one the
    # service accounts cannot read on macOS (home directories are 0750 there).
    # The r44 macOS rehearsal stopped on exactly that: install-backend, run as
    # `_orient` from the operator's own home, failed at pip's own os.getcwd(), after
    # both tokens had rotated. A relative --system-dir still names what the
    # operator typed, so it is resolved before the move.
    if args.system_dir:
        args.system_dir = os.path.abspath(args.system_dir)
    os.chdir("/")

    platform = "macos" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux"
    agents_home_str = account_home(AGENTS)
    if agents_home_str is None:
        print(f"{PROG}: no {AGENTS} account on this machine — nothing to migrate.")
        return 0
    agents_home = Path(agents_home_str)

    pending = needs_migration(agents_home)

    if args.check or args.status:
        if pending:
            print(f"{PROG}: Orient's state is still under {AGENTS} — migration is pending")
        else:
            print(f"{PROG}: nothing pending — Orient is not running as {AGENTS}")
        return 1 if (args.check and pending) else 0

    if args.dry_run:
        if pending:
            print(f"{PROG}: would stop {', '.join(ORIENT_UNIT_LABELS)}, remove "
                  f"{OLD_DEPLOY_WATCH_LABEL}, place Orient's code, rotate "
                  f"{' and '.join(ORIENT_TOKEN_NAMES)}, move "
                  f"{agents_orient_control(agents_home)} and {agents_home / ORIENT_CONFIG_REL} "
                  f"to the {orient_account_name(platform)} account, create its statement key, "
                  f"install the document service, restart Orient, start "
                  f"{ROOT_DEPLOY_UNIT_LABEL}, and repoint cloudflared")
        else:
            print(f"{PROG}: nothing to do — Orient is not running as {AGENTS}")
        return 0

    # Checked here, in the real-run path alone, before anything changes —
    # never for --check/--status/--dry-run, which read and change nothing
    # regardless of where this copy sits. A run from outside its own
    # extracted tree gets past every check below (cloudflared, the two
    # token rotations, every file move) and used to only fail once
    # place_deployed_commit() loaded install-privileged, whose own
    # load-time guard refuses past the point of no return. See CQ·131.
    # Loading that sibling here, too, through
    # _install_privileged_for_extract(), brings its ownership check of
    # the extracted tree forward to this same point, before
    # place_deployed_commit() needs to load it for real.
    problem = script_location_problem(HERE_FILE)
    if not problem:
        try:
            _install_privileged_for_extract()
        except SystemExit as exc:
            problem = ("install-privileged's own location check refused loading it "
                       f"(exit {exc.code})")
    if problem:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing to run from {HERE_FILE}: {problem}. Run "
              f"sudo {root_interpreter(platform)} -I "
              f"<prefix>/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/{PROG} --shape {args.shape} "
              f"--deploy-signals-bound instead. Nothing was migrated.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if not pending:
        print(f"{PROG}: nothing to migrate — Orient is not running as {AGENTS}")
        return 0

    if not args.deploy_signals_bound:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — pass --deploy-signals-bound once Orient's crew has bound "
              f"the instruction block that reads deploy_failure and deploy_stall to its own "
              f"sessions. This script cannot verify that binding itself. Nothing was "
              f"migrated.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    operator = args.operator or os.environ.get("SUDO_USER")
    if not operator:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — could not determine the operator's login; pass --operator. "
              f"Nothing was migrated.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    person_save_sources = None
    if args.root_ca and args.app_key and args.app_config and args.persons:
        person_save_sources = (args.root_ca, args.app_key, args.app_config,
                               [p.strip() for p in args.persons.split(",")])

    try:
        migrate(agents_home, args.shape, platform, operator=operator,
               cloudflared_label=args.cloudflared_label, system_dir=args.system_dir,
               person_save_sources=person_save_sources)
    except Refused as exc:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
